{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_00_cqu1Nprbcmm9pLA0fCQr5129","name":"read_file","content":"[range 1-500 of 4060 lines]\n1\n00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,120\nAll right, let's get started.\n\n2\n00:00:15,180 --> 00:00:16,700\nWelcome back to 006.\n\n3\n00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:20,180\nToday we are doing some of the coolest data structures we will see in this class,\n\n4\n00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:23,900\nmaybe some of the coolest data structures ever, binary trees.\n\n5\n00:00:24,680 --> 00:00:29,140\nYou've certainly seen trees in many forms in the past,\n\n6\n00:00:29,140 --> 00:00:35,620\nincluding in this class we've used trees as a lower bound tool in the decision tree model.\n\n7\n00:00:36,260 --> 00:00:41,200\nBut this lecture and the next lecture we're going to build one data structure\n\n8\n00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:45,200\nthat is almost superior to all data structures we have seen\n\n9\n00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:48,840\nand can do almost anything really fast.\n\n10\n00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:52,860\nFirst, recall all the data structures we've seen so far,\n\n11\n00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:56,760\narrays, linked lists, dynamic arrays, sorted arrays, hash tables.\n\n12\n00:00:57,200 --> 00:00:58,760\nAnd the two...\n\n13\n00:00:59,140 --> 00:01:01,380\nsets of operations we're interested in supporting.\n\n14\n00:01:01,380 --> 00:01:05,840\nThe two interfaces, one was sequences, where we're maintaining items in a specified order.\n\n15\n00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:08,740\nWe want to be able to insert an item right after another item,\n\n16\n00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:10,540\nor delete an item in the middle of the list,\n\n17\n00:01:10,540 --> 00:01:12,540\nand always be able to access the ith item.\n\n18\n00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:15,940\nWe haven't seen any good data structures for that problem.\n\n19\n00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:21,040\nWe're really good at inserting and deleting at the beginning or the end of the sequence,\n\n20\n00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:26,240\nbut we haven't seen anything that's efficient at inserting in the middle of the list\n\n21\n00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:27,740\nor deleting in the middle of the list.\n\n22\n00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:28,340\nLinked list,\n\n23\n00:01:28,340 --> 00:01:31,340\nyou can't even get to the middle in less than linear time.\n\n24\n00:01:31,340 --> 00:01:34,340\nArray, you can get to the middle, but if you make any changes,\n\n25\n00:01:34,340 --> 00:01:36,340\nyou have to do this shift, which is very expensive.\n\n26\n00:01:36,340 --> 00:01:38,340\nSo today...\n\n27\n00:01:38,340 --> 00:01:44,340\nOr sorry, next lecture, for the first time we will see all of those operations efficient.\n\n28\n00:01:46,340 --> 00:01:48,340\nI'll mention our goal.\n\n29\n00:01:49,340 --> 00:01:52,340\nWhere efficient means logarithmic.\n\n30\n00:01:52,340 --> 00:01:56,340\nSo we're not quite as good as linked lists and dynamic arrays\n\n31\n00:01:56,340 --> 00:01:57,340\nat inserting and deleting.\n\n32\n00:01:57,340 --> 00:01:58,340\nSo we're not quite as good as linked lists and dynamic arrays at inserting and deleting.\n\n33\n00:01:58,340 --> 00:02:00,340\nSo we're not quite as good as linked lists and dynamic arrays at inserting and deleting.\n\n34\n00:02:00,340 --> 00:02:02,340\nAnd so we do this trick when we have full arguments.\n\n35\n00:02:02,340 --> 00:02:02,620\nSo we do the whole\n\n36\n00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:04,280\nAnd so we do the whole\n\n37\n00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:08,340\nFor the first time, we're going to be using this type of algorithm that allows\n\n38\n00:02:08,340 --> 00:02:13,340\nthat we're going to have the best of all worlds where we can solve all the things,\n\n39\n00:02:13,340 --> 00:02:15,340\nall the operations that don't build or iterate through the entire structure.\n\n40\n00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:17,340\nThat of course takes linear time.\n\n41\n00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:19,340\nBut we can do all the others in logarithmic time.\n\n42\n00:02:19,340 --> 00:02:20,340\nFor sets,\n\n43\n00:02:20,340 --> 00:02:24,340\nSets were maintaining a bunch of items,\n\n44\n00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:26,340\nwhich have intrinsic keys.\n\n45\n00:02:26,340 --> 00:02:27,340\nAnd we want to search by key.\n\n46\n00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:32,720\ntables are great if you're only doing exact searches. If you want to find a key and get\n\n47\n00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:37,320\nyes or no, is it in there? And if it's in there, give me the item. That's what Python\n\n48\n00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:44,580\ndictionaries do. And they're great at inserting and deleting. But they're really bad at find\n\n49\n00:02:44,580 --> 00:02:49,780\nprevious and find next. This is the unsuccessful case. If I search for a key and it's not in\n\n50\n00:02:49,780 --> 00:02:54,300\nmy structure, I would like to know more than just the answer no. I'd like to know what\n\n51\n00:02:54,300 --> 00:03:00,540\nthe previous and next items that are actually in the structure. So what are my nearest matches\n\n52\n00:03:00,540 --> 00:03:05,540\nwhen I search by key? That's a natural query. And the only data structure we have that's\n\n53\n00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:10,620\ngood at it is a sorted array because binary search gives this to us. If we search for\n\n54\n00:03:10,620 --> 00:03:16,120\na key by binary search and we don't find it, the position that we end up at is right between\n\n55\n00:03:16,120 --> 00:03:21,620\nthe previous and next one. But of course, sorted arrays are terrible for dynamic operations.\n\n56\n00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:24,060\nWe don't know how to maintain.\n\n57\n00:03:24,060 --> 00:03:24,280\nWe can't maintain the same key. We can't maintain the same key. We can't maintain the same key.\n\n58\n00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:28,620\nWe can't maintain a sorted array without any gaps when we're doing insertions and deletions.\n\n59\n00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:34,720\nIn some sense, today and next class, binary trees let us represent a sorted order or,\n\n60\n00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:40,620\nin general, an order of items dynamically and still allow us to do very fast things\n\n61\n00:03:40,620 --> 00:03:45,900\nlike get at of i and find previous of the key. So that's our goal. We're not going to\n\n62\n00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:52,720\nquite get to this goal today. We're going to get an incomparable thing called the height\n\n63\n00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:54,040\nof the tree.\n\n64\n00:03:54,040 --> 00:03:58,440\nAnd then on Thursday, we'll be able to finish and achieve this goal. Today is just in service\n\n65\n00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:02,579\nto that goal.\n\n66\n00:04:02,579 --> 00:04:13,299\nSo what is a binary tree? Let me draw an example and then define it more precisely.\n\n67\n00:04:13,299 --> 00:04:18,120\nMathematicians will call this a rooted binary tree, in case you've seen that in 042, say.\n\n68\n00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:21,159\nHere is a picture.\n\n69\n00:04:21,159 --> 00:04:21,920\nIt looks like this.\n\n70\n00:04:21,920 --> 00:04:23,080\nIt's a tree, right?\n\n71\n00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:23,380\nYeah.\n\n72\n00:04:23,380 --> 00:04:39,360\nSo, this is an example of a binary tree.\n\n73\n00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:41,540\nIt has a bunch of nodes which we're drawing in circles.\n\n74\n00:04:41,540 --> 00:04:45,800\nIt has items in the nodes which we're writing as letters here.\n\n75\n00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:50,240\nSo this is item A, item B, item C. And it has these links between them.\n\n76\n00:04:50,240 --> 00:04:52,840\nThis is like linked lists.\n\n77\n00:04:52,840 --> 00:05:06,120\nBut in general, a node x is going to have a parent pointer, a left child, left pointer,\n\n78\n00:05:06,120 --> 00:05:09,599\nand a right child, right pointer.\n\n79\n00:05:09,599 --> 00:05:14,280\nAnd it also has an item inside of it.\n\n80\n00:05:14,280 --> 00:05:21,200\nSo I'm going to talk about node.left is a pointer to the node down here.\n\n81\n00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:22,780\nNode.right, node.parent, node.ident.\n\n82\n00:05:22,780 --> 00:05:22,840\nOkay.\n\n83\n00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:23,840\nSo node.right, node.parent, node.ident.\n\n84\n00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:31,479\nSo if I look at the node A, its item is A.\n\n85\n00:05:31,479 --> 00:05:37,960\nSo let me draw for you some examples.\n\n86\n00:05:52,840 --> 00:05:53,840\nOkay.\n\n87\n00:05:53,840 --> 00:05:56,940\nThe parent of A is nothing.\n\n88\n00:05:56,940 --> 00:05:58,479\nSo we call A the root node.\n\n89\n00:05:58,479 --> 00:06:01,760\nThere's going to be a unique node that has no parent.\n\n90\n00:06:01,760 --> 00:06:05,799\nIt's sad to have no parents, but there you go.\n\n91\n00:06:05,799 --> 00:06:11,080\nThen we have node B, whose parent is A. Node C, its parent is A.\n\n92\n00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:14,799\nNode D, its parent is B. Node E, its parent is B.\n\n93\n00:06:14,799 --> 00:06:20,840\nAnd node F, its parent is D. The alphabetical order here happens to be ordered by parent.\n\n94\n00:06:20,840 --> 00:06:21,840\nSo we're going to have a parent.\n\n95\n00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:22,840\nAnd then we're going to have a parent.\n\n96\n00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:24,520\nThen we have left pointers.\n\n97\n00:06:24,520 --> 00:06:25,900\nI'll just do a few of them.\n\n98\n00:06:25,900 --> 00:06:32,599\nSo left pointer of A is B. Right pointer of A, sorry, B, the node.\n\n99\n00:06:32,599 --> 00:06:37,200\nThese should all be nodes.\n\n100\n00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:40,400\nI'm circling for nodes and just writing the letter for the item.\n\n101\n00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:42,820\nMake it clear that those are different things.\n\n102\n00:06:42,820 --> 00:06:50,200\nThe right pointer for A is C. Left pointer for B is D. Right pointer for B is E. And\n\n103\n00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:51,200\nso on.\n\n104\n00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:52,200\nOkay.\n\n105\n00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:52,780\nSo in other words, each of these lines are going to be a node.\n\n106\n00:06:52,780 --> 00:06:55,280\nLet's try to ق how it works, is that clear?\n\n107\n00:06:55,280 --> 00:06:57,080\nThat's a hard one, honestly.\n\n108\n00:06:57,080 --> 00:07:05,119\nYou have got to remember there's a very\n\n109\n00:07:05,119 --> 00:07:10,419\nThere's a to이죠t�� answer here.\n\n110\n00:07:10,419 --> 00:07:15,700\nSo this is aникātnobj to 어�e walked on commonANK.\n\n111\n00:07:15,700 --> 00:07:16,820\nI'll go back to the source item, so that we can connect.\n\n112\n00:07:16,820 --> 00:07:18,859\nSo you've got to make lines including the other ones inside this node.\n\n113\n00:07:18,859 --> 00:07:19,859\nSo what we had was we can kind of connect these and push the Linux sites around here.\n\n114\n00:07:19,859 --> 00:07:20,859\nThere's nightmare counter, right?\n\n115\n00:07:20,859 --> 00:07:21,859\nTosugane component link window covering the Alpha shape.\n\n116\n00:07:21,859 --> 00:07:22,559\nWe would like to call those, right?\n\n117\n00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:25,500\nThis should be the same as node.\n\n118\n00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:28,300\nSo that's just saying parent is always\n\n119\n00:07:28,300 --> 00:07:30,660\nthe inverse of a left or right operation.\n\n120\n00:07:30,660 --> 00:07:32,040\nThis is also true for right.\n\n121\n00:07:35,020 --> 00:07:36,120\nAnd that's a binary tree.\n\n122\n00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:39,840\nNow the intuition of what's going on here is,\n\n123\n00:07:39,840 --> 00:07:43,860\nyou could say we're inspired by a linked list.\n\n124\n00:07:43,860 --> 00:07:47,520\nLinked list had a very similar structure.\n\n125\n00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:52,020\nMaybe an item or there's a node, it had an item in it,\n"}
