{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_00_wdbnJHJuhEUvb5hEsXYG2176","name":"read_file","content":"[range 501-1000 of 4060 lines]\n126\n00:07:52,020 --> 00:07:56,780\nand it had a next pointer, and it had a previous pointer.\n\n127\n00:07:56,780 --> 00:07:58,780\nSo in some sense, if it's doubly linked,\n\n128\n00:07:58,780 --> 00:07:59,900\nwe had a previous pointer.\n\n129\n00:07:59,900 --> 00:08:02,560\nIf it was singly linked, we only had a next pointer.\n\n130\n00:08:02,560 --> 00:08:06,180\nAnd if you think about the limits of linked lists,\n\n131\n00:08:06,180 --> 00:08:08,440\nespecially singly linked lists, if you just\n\n132\n00:08:08,440 --> 00:08:12,260\nhave one pointer per node, you can only build a list.\n\n133\n00:08:12,260 --> 00:08:19,680\nAnd so the result is this node is\n\n134\n00:08:19,680 --> 00:08:21,719\ngoing to have depth linear.\n\n135\n00:08:22,020 --> 00:08:25,960\nDepth means how many pointers do I have to follow to get here\n\n136\n00:08:25,960 --> 00:08:28,500\nfrom the root of the structure, which for linked lists\n\n137\n00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:29,560\nwas the head.\n\n138\n00:08:29,560 --> 00:08:30,479\nThis is doubly linked.\n\n139\n00:08:30,479 --> 00:08:32,240\nOK, I can have a head and a tail,\n\n140\n00:08:32,240 --> 00:08:34,500\nand I can put bidirections on here.\n\n141\n00:08:34,500 --> 00:08:36,980\nBut then still the middle item has depth linear.\n\n142\n00:08:36,980 --> 00:08:40,000\nSo there's no way to get there in less than linear time.\n\n143\n00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:44,000\nWith binary trees, because we use two types of next pointers,\n\n144\n00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,760\nleft and right, we can build a tree.\n\n145\n00:08:46,760 --> 00:08:50,780\nAnd we know trees in general can have logarithmic height.\n\n146\n00:08:50,780 --> 00:08:51,900\nThe point is that we can build a tree, and we know trees in general have logarithmic height.\n\n147\n00:08:51,900 --> 00:08:56,620\nAnd so it's possible in a tree to get to any node starting\n\n148\n00:08:56,620 --> 00:08:59,020\nfrom the root in only log n traversals.\n\n149\n00:08:59,020 --> 00:09:01,220\nSo that's the intuition on what's going on.\n\n150\n00:09:01,220 --> 00:09:08,240\nNow today, we're going to talk about the height of a tree.\n\n151\n00:09:08,240 --> 00:09:09,140\nSo let me define.\n\n152\n00:09:12,660 --> 00:09:14,660\nI'm going to need a couple of definitions here.\n\n153\n00:09:18,660 --> 00:09:20,520\nSubtree and height.\n\n154\n00:09:21,900 --> 00:09:24,660\nSo a tree decomposes into subtrees.\n\n155\n00:09:24,660 --> 00:09:30,660\nSo for example, the subtree rooted at b, or the subtree of b,\n\n156\n00:09:30,660 --> 00:09:34,660\nis this portion of the tree.\n\n157\n00:09:34,660 --> 00:09:38,660\nSo it's that node and all of the descendants of this node.\n\n158\n00:09:38,660 --> 00:09:40,660\nSo because we have parents and children,\n\n159\n00:09:40,660 --> 00:09:44,660\nwe can generalize in the familial tree sense.\n\n160\n00:09:44,660 --> 00:09:47,660\nWe can talk about ancestors of a node.\n\n161\n00:09:47,660 --> 00:09:50,660\nWe can talk about the descendants of a node.\n\n162\n00:09:50,660 --> 00:09:51,699\nWe can talk about the descendants of a node.\n\n163\n00:09:51,700 --> 00:09:55,460\nSo the ancestors of f are its parent, its grandparent,\n\n164\n00:09:55,460 --> 00:09:57,460\nits great-grandparents, and so on.\n\n165\n00:09:57,460 --> 00:10:01,840\nTogether, all of these are called ancestors.\n\n166\n00:10:01,840 --> 00:10:04,160\nIt doesn't quite correspond to familial trees,\n\n167\n00:10:04,160 --> 00:10:06,740\nbecause familial trees, you have two parents.\n\n168\n00:10:06,740 --> 00:10:10,220\nHere, you only have a unique parent,\n\n169\n00:10:10,220 --> 00:10:13,360\nor the poor root has no parent.\n\n170\n00:10:13,360 --> 00:10:16,660\nWe also talk about, it's like mixed metaphors,\n\n171\n00:10:16,660 --> 00:10:19,700\nleaves of the tree.\n\n172\n00:10:19,700 --> 00:10:20,200\nThese are.\n\n173\n00:10:21,700 --> 00:10:26,460\nPeople with no children.\n\n174\n00:10:26,460 --> 00:10:27,840\nParents will complain about this.\n\n175\n00:10:27,840 --> 00:10:32,940\nBut like many of us in this room, we have no children yet.\n\n176\n00:10:32,940 --> 00:10:34,620\nSo we are called leaves.\n\n177\n00:10:34,620 --> 00:10:37,600\nYou can tell your parent, hey, I'm just a leaf, blowing\n\n178\n00:10:37,600 --> 00:10:39,580\nin the wind.\n\n179\n00:10:39,580 --> 00:10:43,240\nSo it's so many mixed metaphors.\n\n180\n00:10:43,240 --> 00:10:46,400\nBut we always draw trees downwards,\n\n181\n00:10:46,400 --> 00:10:48,900\nlike the root structure of a tree.\n\n182\n00:10:48,900 --> 00:10:51,700\nYet we call the ends of the roots leaves.\n\n183\n00:10:51,700 --> 00:10:52,820\nWhich is upside down.\n\n184\n00:10:52,820 --> 00:10:55,660\nAnyway, that's trees for you.\n\n185\n00:10:55,660 --> 00:10:57,480\nLots of entertaining analogies.\n\n186\n00:10:57,480 --> 00:10:58,740\nBut ancestors are useful.\n\n187\n00:10:58,740 --> 00:11:00,160\nDescendants are also useful.\n\n188\n00:11:00,160 --> 00:11:02,820\nSo the descendants of b are all of its children,\n\n189\n00:11:02,820 --> 00:11:05,020\nand all of its grandchildren, and all the way down,\n\n190\n00:11:05,020 --> 00:11:07,200\nbut just within the subtree.\n\n191\n00:11:07,200 --> 00:11:12,140\nSo subtree of x consists of x and its descendants.\n\n192\n00:11:17,040 --> 00:11:20,460\nAnd we think of x being the root of that subtree.\n\n193\n00:11:20,460 --> 00:11:21,460\nSo we think of x being the root of that subtree.\n\n194\n00:11:21,700 --> 00:11:23,820\nSo we're kind of forgetting about everything\n\n195\n00:11:23,820 --> 00:11:28,240\noutside of the subtree when we talk about subtree of x.\n\n196\n00:11:28,240 --> 00:11:30,480\nLet's talk about the depth of a node.\n\n197\n00:11:33,480 --> 00:11:39,160\nDepth of the node is, I guess, the number of its ancestors.\n\n198\n00:11:45,880 --> 00:11:51,000\nThe way I usually think of it is the number of edges,\n\n199\n00:11:51,700 --> 00:11:52,940\nthe depth of the node.\n\n200\n00:11:52,940 --> 00:11:59,900\nAnd the path from x up to the root.\n\n201\n00:12:02,620 --> 00:12:06,320\nSo every node has a unique path that goes upwards\n\n202\n00:12:06,320 --> 00:12:08,320\nuntil it can't go up anymore.\n\n203\n00:12:08,320 --> 00:12:14,060\nSo the depth of e is 2 because there are two edges, one, two,\n\n204\n00:12:14,060 --> 00:12:16,540\nin the path from the root a to e.\n\n205\n00:12:16,540 --> 00:12:18,940\nSo maybe I'll write some depths.\n\n206\n00:12:18,940 --> 00:12:20,480\nDepth of e is 2.\n\n207\n00:12:20,480 --> 00:12:20,900\nDepth of these guys is 1.\n\n208\n00:12:20,900 --> 00:12:20,920\nDepth of these guys is 2.\n\n209\n00:12:20,920 --> 00:12:21,400\nSo the depth of e is 2.\n\n210\n00:12:21,400 --> 00:12:21,480\nSo the depth of e is 2.\n\n211\n00:12:21,480 --> 00:12:21,680\nDepth of these guys is 1.\n\n212\n00:12:21,680 --> 00:12:24,560\nDepth of the root is 0.\n\n213\n00:12:24,560 --> 00:12:26,780\n2, 3.\n\n214\n00:12:26,780 --> 00:12:31,180\nSo those are depths.\n\n215\n00:12:31,180 --> 00:12:34,260\nI'm going to clean this up a little bit.\n\n216\n00:12:39,520 --> 00:12:40,979\nFocus on the image.\n\n217\n00:12:40,979 --> 00:12:41,939\nAll right, height.\n\n218\n00:12:41,939 --> 00:12:44,939\nSo depth is measuring downwards because if you\n\n219\n00:12:44,939 --> 00:12:49,540\nimagine depth within water, this is the surface of the water.\n\n220\n00:12:49,540 --> 00:12:51,459\nAnd then we measure how deep you are from the surface.\n\n221\n00:12:51,460 --> 00:12:54,220\nHeight is in the reverse direction.\n\n222\n00:12:54,220 --> 00:12:56,460\nWe're going to measure from the leaf level up\n\n223\n00:12:56,460 --> 00:12:58,960\nbecause leaves are the bottom of the tree.\n\n224\n00:12:58,960 --> 00:13:07,300\nSo height of a node is going to be the number of edges\n\n225\n00:13:07,300 --> 00:13:21,240\nin the longest downward path, which is the same thing.\n\n226\n00:13:21,240 --> 00:13:26,160\nSo height of a node is going to be the maximum depth of a node\n\n227\n00:13:26,160 --> 00:13:27,320\nin x's subtree.\n\n228\n00:13:32,920 --> 00:13:37,160\nLet's do height in red.\n\n229\n00:13:37,160 --> 00:13:39,800\nSo how long is the longest path from f to a leaf?\n\n230\n00:13:39,800 --> 00:13:40,640\nWell, f is a leaf.\n\n231\n00:13:40,640 --> 00:13:44,360\nSo all leaves have depth 0.\n\n232\n00:13:44,360 --> 00:13:45,960\nSorry, height 0.\n\n233\n00:13:45,960 --> 00:13:46,860\nI'll get it backwards.\n\n234\n00:13:46,860 --> 00:13:50,360\nD here.\n\n235\n00:13:50,360 --> 00:13:50,900\nIt's.\n\n236\n00:13:50,900 --> 00:13:53,140\nSo there are two ways to go down.\n\n237\n00:13:53,140 --> 00:13:55,220\nThis doesn't go to a leaf.\n\n238\n00:13:55,220 --> 00:13:56,400\nThis one goes to a leaf.\n\n239\n00:13:56,400 --> 00:13:58,140\nAnd its height is 0.\n\n240\n00:13:58,140 --> 00:13:59,740\nSo this height is 1.\n\n241\n00:13:59,740 --> 00:14:02,400\nThere's one edge to a leaf here.\n\n242\n00:14:02,400 --> 00:14:04,720\nB has two leaves it can get to.\n\n243\n00:14:04,720 --> 00:14:05,819\nWe take the longest one.\n\n244\n00:14:05,819 --> 00:14:08,240\nSo that's length 2.\n\n245\n00:14:08,240 --> 00:14:10,840\nA similarly has height 3.\n\n246\n00:14:16,220 --> 00:14:16,720\nOK.\n\n247\n00:14:16,720 --> 00:14:19,180\nSo height we measure upward.\n\n248\n00:14:19,180 --> 00:14:20,520\nDepth we measure downward.\n\n249\n00:14:20,520 --> 00:14:21,520\nDownward.\n\n250\n00:14:21,520 --> 00:14:23,980\nWell, one thing we care about is just\n"}
{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_00_sC7oT70Cm1jGr4x1xeJd6956","type":"function","function":{"name":"read_file","arguments":"{\"range\": \"1001-1500\", \"path\": \"网络共享文件夹/9-6. Binary Trees, Part 1-720P 准高清-AVC.realcugan (transcribed on 23-May-2026 22-42-31).srt\"}"}},{"id":"call_01_94tGI9BP33bqTRDKe6624211","type":"function","function":{"name":"read_file","arguments":"{\"range\": \"1501-2000\", \"path\": \"网络共享文件夹/9-6. Binary Trees, Part 1-720P 准高清-AVC.realcugan (transcribed on 23-May-2026 22-42-31).srt\"}"}},{"id":"call_02_vyw6NWlgjlC1bsr25OuC1786","type":"function","function":{"name":"read_file","arguments":"{\"range\": \"2001-2500\", \"path\": \"网络共享文件夹/9-6. Binary Trees, Part 1-720P 准高清-AVC.realcugan (transcribed on 23-May-2026 22-42-31).srt\"}"}},{"id":"call_03_TOPQnhg4rdUrbXxW0yWh4167","type":"function","function":{"name":"read_file","arguments":"{\"range\": \"2501-3000\", \"path\": \"网络共享文件夹/9-6. Binary Trees, Part 1-720P 准高清-AVC.realcugan (transcribed on 23-May-2026 22-42-31).srt\"}"}},{"id":"call_04_moC27Z2lwRSeQ5RmMDTk3020","type":"function","function":{"name":"read_file","arguments":"{\"range\": \"3001-3500\", \"path\": \"网络共享文件夹/9-6. Binary Trees, Part 1-720P 准高清-AVC.realcugan (transcribed on 23-May-2026 22-42-31).srt\"}"}},{"id":"call_05_ubXESxDVdp4qLb289AP93977","type":"function","function":{"name":"read_file","arguments":"{\"range\": \"3501-4060\", \"path\": \"网络共享文件夹/9-6. Binary Trees, Part 1-720P 准高清-AVC.realcugan (transcribed on 23-May-2026 22-42-31).srt\"}"}}],"reasoning_content":"Let me continue reading the rest of the file. I have 1-500 and 501-1000 so far. Let me read 1001-1500, 1501-2000, etc."}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_00_sC7oT70Cm1jGr4x1xeJd6956","name":"read_file","content":"[range 1001-1500 of 4060 lines]\n251\n00:14:23,980 --> 00:14:29,840\nthe height of the overall tree, which is the height of the root.\n\n252\n00:14:32,840 --> 00:14:34,400\nAnd I'm going to call that h because we're\n\n253\n00:14:34,400 --> 00:14:35,280\ngoing to use it a lot.\n\n254\n00:14:39,620 --> 00:14:41,319\nAnd what we're going to achieve today\n\n255\n00:14:41,319 --> 00:14:44,400\nis all of these running times, instead of being log n,\n\n256\n00:14:44,400 --> 00:14:46,020\nthey're going to be order h.\n\n257\n00:14:48,760 --> 00:14:49,819\nSo today, our goal is to get to the root of the root.\n\n258\n00:14:49,820 --> 00:14:53,280\nAnd then the goal is to get all the operations we care about\n\n259\n00:14:53,280 --> 00:14:54,480\nin order h time.\n\n260\n00:14:57,260 --> 00:14:58,900\nAnd then next lecture, we're going\n\n261\n00:14:58,900 --> 00:15:01,860\nto guarantee that h is always log n.\n\n262\n00:15:01,860 --> 00:15:03,780\nAnd then we'll get log n time.\n\n263\n00:15:03,780 --> 00:15:05,740\nSo we need to do a bunch of work to achieve log n.\n\n264\n00:15:05,740 --> 00:15:08,700\nToday, we'll do the work that's all the tree manipulation.\n\n265\n00:15:08,700 --> 00:15:11,140\nAnd as long as your tree is nice and shallow,\n\n266\n00:15:11,140 --> 00:15:12,880\nit doesn't have high height.\n\n267\n00:15:12,880 --> 00:15:15,580\nIf it has logarithmic height, everything will be log n.\n\n268\n00:15:15,580 --> 00:15:17,820\nOf course, there are trees that are really bad.\n\n269\n00:15:17,820 --> 00:15:18,520\nAll right.\n\n270\n00:15:18,520 --> 00:15:19,820\nWe can have a tree.\n\n271\n00:15:19,820 --> 00:15:29,360\nA tree like this, which is basically a linked list,\n\n272\n00:15:29,360 --> 00:15:31,940\nwhere we only use right pointers and all the left pointers\n\n273\n00:15:31,940 --> 00:15:32,820\nare none.\n\n274\n00:15:32,820 --> 00:15:37,940\nSo there are trees that are very high, have high height.\n\n275\n00:15:37,940 --> 00:15:40,020\nWe want to avoid these, but we won't worry about that\n\n276\n00:15:40,020 --> 00:15:40,780\nuntil next lecture.\n\n277\n00:15:40,780 --> 00:15:41,280\nQuestion?\n\n278\n00:15:41,280 --> 00:15:45,580\nThe question is, is the height of node c zero?\n\n279\n00:15:45,580 --> 00:15:47,080\nWhat is the height of node c?\n\n280\n00:15:47,080 --> 00:15:49,300\nHeight of node c is zero, because the length, which is,\n\n281\n00:15:49,300 --> 00:15:49,660\nI think, the height of node c is zero.\n\n282\n00:15:49,660 --> 00:15:49,760\nThe height of node c is zero.\n\n283\n00:15:49,760 --> 00:15:49,800\nThe height of node c is zero.\n\n284\n00:15:49,800 --> 00:15:50,740\nThe height of node c is zero.\n\n285\n00:15:50,740 --> 00:15:51,339\nThe height of node c is zero.\n\n286\n00:15:51,339 --> 00:15:51,740\nThe height of node c is zero.\n\n287\n00:15:51,740 --> 00:15:52,300\nThe height of node c is zero.\n\n288\n00:15:52,300 --> 00:15:54,439\nAnd the number of edges in the longest downward path is zero.\n\n289\n00:15:54,439 --> 00:15:54,859\nYeah.\n\n290\n00:15:54,859 --> 00:15:56,219\nWe're counting edges, not vertices.\n\n291\n00:16:01,540 --> 00:16:03,819\nSo the height of the tree is, of course, just the depth.\n\n292\n00:16:03,819 --> 00:16:05,640\nThe maximum depth, I think that's right.\n\n293\n00:16:05,640 --> 00:16:08,479\nSo the height here is 3.\n\n294\n00:16:08,479 --> 00:16:11,479\nAnd the maximum depth is this terribly drawn 3.\n\n295\n00:16:11,479 --> 00:16:14,520\nSo these happen to correspond in the maximum case.\n\n296\n00:16:14,520 --> 00:16:17,459\nBut we use height to always mean maximum.\n\n297\n00:16:17,459 --> 00:16:19,740\nAnd so that's why we talk about the height\n\n298\n00:16:19,740 --> 00:16:20,820\nof the tree.\n\n299\n00:16:20,820 --> 00:16:25,060\nDepth of the tree is not defined, just depths of nodes.\n\n300\n00:16:25,060 --> 00:16:27,740\nOK.\n\n301\n00:16:27,740 --> 00:16:30,720\nHow do we use these trees to represent either a sequence\n\n302\n00:16:30,720 --> 00:16:33,440\nor a set?\n\n303\n00:16:33,440 --> 00:16:37,720\nI claim that there is a natural order in trees called\n\n304\n00:16:37,720 --> 00:16:49,560\nthe traversal order of nodes or items.\n\n305\n00:16:49,740 --> 00:16:53,180\nNodes in the tree.\n\n306\n00:16:53,180 --> 00:16:57,920\nSo I'm going to define a particular order,\n\n307\n00:16:57,920 --> 00:16:59,980\nsay, in this example.\n\n308\n00:16:59,980 --> 00:17:01,220\nLet's do the example first.\n\n309\n00:17:04,140 --> 00:17:19,720\nThe traversal order is going to be f, d, b, e, a,\n\n310\n00:17:19,740 --> 00:17:20,240\nc.\n\n311\n00:17:20,240 --> 00:17:22,740\nI feel like I'm in music class.\n\n312\n00:17:22,740 --> 00:17:25,500\nThis is my guitar or something, but it's not.\n\n313\n00:17:25,500 --> 00:17:27,079\nI hope.\n\n314\n00:17:27,079 --> 00:17:29,220\nIf it is, it's a coincidence.\n\n315\n00:17:29,220 --> 00:17:32,100\nSo what is this order?\n\n316\n00:17:32,100 --> 00:17:36,760\nWhat I'd like to do is recursively define an order\n\n317\n00:17:36,760 --> 00:17:39,720\nwhere the root of the tree is somewhere in the middle,\n\n318\n00:17:39,720 --> 00:17:41,840\nand everything in the left subtree\n\n319\n00:17:41,840 --> 00:17:44,940\nis left earlier in the order than the root,\n\n320\n00:17:44,940 --> 00:17:47,300\nand everything in the right subtree is later.\n\n321\n00:17:47,300 --> 00:17:48,140\nSo you can see here, c comes after a.\n\n322\n00:17:48,140 --> 00:17:48,640\nAnd then, c comes after a.\n\n323\n00:17:48,640 --> 00:17:49,340\nSo I want to define a sub-tree.\n\n324\n00:17:49,340 --> 00:17:49,640\nAnd then, I want to define a sub-tree.\n\n325\n00:17:49,640 --> 00:17:49,720\nAnd then, I want to define a sub-tree.\n\n326\n00:17:49,740 --> 00:17:52,820\nAnd then, all the other nodes come before a.\n\n327\n00:17:52,820 --> 00:17:56,160\nAnd recursively, if I look at a node b,\n\n328\n00:17:56,160 --> 00:18:00,540\nthis node b, which appears over here, e is to the right of it.\n\n329\n00:18:00,540 --> 00:18:02,940\nBut this is all to the left of a.\n\n330\n00:18:02,940 --> 00:18:07,160\nSo e is between b and a, b on the left.\n\n331\n00:18:07,160 --> 00:18:09,180\nAnd then, f and d are to the left of that.\n\n332\n00:18:09,180 --> 00:18:11,760\nAnd again, f comes before d, because f\n\n333\n00:18:11,760 --> 00:18:15,140\nis in the left subtree of d.\n\n334\n00:18:15,140 --> 00:18:19,640\nSo we say, for every node.\n\n335\n00:18:19,640 --> 00:18:31,640\nThe nodes in x dot left are before x.\n\n336\n00:18:31,640 --> 00:18:38,640\nAnd the nodes in x dot right come after x.\n\n337\n00:18:38,640 --> 00:18:46,520\nAnd this uniquely defines an order called a traversal order.\n\n338\n00:18:46,520 --> 00:18:49,320\nIt's also called the in-order traversal order.\n\n339\n00:18:49,320 --> 00:18:51,939\nIt's called in-order because it's in the traversal order.\n\n340\n00:18:51,939 --> 00:18:52,939\nSo it's very circular.\n\n341\n00:18:52,939 --> 00:18:55,780\nBut you may have seen in-order traversal.\n\n342\n00:18:55,780 --> 00:18:57,439\nThis is the same thing.\n\n343\n00:18:57,439 --> 00:19:00,919\nThere's a very simple algorithm for computing this.\n\n344\n00:19:00,919 --> 00:19:07,020\nIf I want to iterate, let's call this, yeah.\n\n345\n00:19:07,020 --> 00:19:11,439\nIf I want to iterate all the nodes within a subtree x,\n\n346\n00:19:11,439 --> 00:19:17,020\nrooted by x, I just iterate on all of the nodes\n\n347\n00:19:17,020 --> 00:19:18,919\nin the left subtree.\n\n348\n00:19:18,920 --> 00:19:23,000\nThen I output x itself.\n\n349\n00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:26,000\nThen I iterate on all the nodes in the right subtree.\n\n350\n00:19:30,920 --> 00:19:31,420\nOK.\n\n351\n00:19:31,420 --> 00:19:33,460\nYou may have seen that algorithm before.\n\n352\n00:19:33,460 --> 00:19:35,980\nThis is just another way to codify the same thing.\n\n353\n00:19:35,980 --> 00:19:38,260\nThe result is all the nodes within a subtree\n\n354\n00:19:38,260 --> 00:19:41,040\nappear continuously with no interruptions.\n\n355\n00:19:41,040 --> 00:19:44,440\nAnd then the parent's going to come before or after,\n\n356\n00:19:44,440 --> 00:19:46,600\ndepending on whether this is the left or right child.\n\n357\n00:19:46,600 --> 00:19:47,100\nOK.\n\n358\n00:19:47,100 --> 00:19:48,040\nSo that's the way to do it.\n\n359\n00:19:48,040 --> 00:19:48,540\nOK.\n\n360\n00:19:48,540 --> 00:19:52,460\nSo and now it's just a matter of connecting the dots.\n\n361\n00:19:52,460 --> 00:19:54,820\nBecause we're representing an order.\n\n362\n00:19:54,820 --> 00:19:57,659\nAnd for sequence, that is going to be the sequence order.\n\n363\n00:19:57,659 --> 00:20:00,379\nIf we want to store n items x0 through x1,\n\n364\n00:20:00,379 --> 00:20:03,040\nwe're going to build some kind of tree.\n\n365\n00:20:03,040 --> 00:20:07,040\nWe're going to put x0 here, x1 here, x2 here, x3 here, x4 here,\n\n366\n00:20:07,040 --> 00:20:08,440\nx5 here.\n\n367\n00:20:08,440 --> 00:20:10,840\nYou can see I'm very used to dealing with traversal orders.\n\n368\n00:20:10,840 --> 00:20:12,159\nIt takes a little while.\n\n369\n00:20:12,159 --> 00:20:13,280\nYou could also see it here.\n\n370\n00:20:13,280 --> 00:20:17,020\nWe're going to put x1 in this node, x2, sorry, x0 here, x1\n\n371\n00:20:17,020 --> 00:20:18,540\nhere, x2 here, and so on.\n\n372\n00:20:18,540 --> 00:20:21,760\nThat's the same order that I gave.\n\n373\n00:20:21,760 --> 00:20:22,260\nOK.\n\n374\n00:20:22,260 --> 00:20:23,060\nThat's for sequences.\n\n375\n00:20:23,060 --> 00:20:26,020\nFor sets, that order is just going to be the sorted order.\n"}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_01_94tGI9BP33bqTRDKe6624211","name":"read_file","content":"[range 1501-2000 of 4060 lines]\n376\n00:20:26,020 --> 00:20:27,899\nAnd we're going to be effectively representing\n\n377\n00:20:27,899 --> 00:20:31,840\nthe sorted order of keys, say, increasing.\n\n378\n00:20:31,840 --> 00:20:36,520\nBut before we get to that, let's talk about different operations\n\n379\n00:20:36,520 --> 00:20:44,100\nwe can do just playing around with traversal order.\n\n380\n00:20:44,100 --> 00:20:47,000\nAnd then we're going to use these to build the sequence\n\n381\n00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:48,540\nand set operations that we care about.\n\n382\n00:20:48,540 --> 00:20:54,620\nSo first operation, I'll call subtree first.\n\n383\n00:20:54,620 --> 00:20:56,420\nSeems appropriate that it's called first.\n\n384\n00:20:56,420 --> 00:21:00,540\nThis is the first one.\n\n385\n00:21:00,540 --> 00:21:10,200\nSo given a node, which I'll call node, this defines a subtree,\n\n386\n00:21:10,200 --> 00:21:13,780\nwhich usually we draw subtrees as triangles hanging off\n\n387\n00:21:13,780 --> 00:21:16,300\nof the node.\n\n388\n00:21:16,300 --> 00:21:17,379\nSo here, I would write n.\n\n389\n00:21:17,379 --> 00:21:17,780\nOK.\n\n390\n00:21:17,780 --> 00:21:18,220\nAll right.\n\n391\n00:21:18,220 --> 00:21:18,300\nOK.\n\n392\n00:21:18,300 --> 00:21:18,320\nAll right.\n\n393\n00:21:18,320 --> 00:21:18,460\nAll right.\n\n394\n00:21:18,460 --> 00:21:18,540\nAll right.\n\n395\n00:21:18,540 --> 00:21:19,540\nSo I'm going to call this subtrees.\n\n396\n00:21:19,540 --> 00:21:23,300\nAnd then there's some subtree of all the descendants of x.\n\n397\n00:21:23,300 --> 00:21:25,420\nSo with subtree first, I would like\n\n398\n00:21:25,420 --> 00:21:35,920\nto say among all the nodes in this subtree, which comes first\n\n399\n00:21:35,920 --> 00:21:37,000\nin traversal order?\n\n400\n00:21:43,300 --> 00:21:47,659\nSo just restricting to that subtree.\n\n401\n00:21:47,659 --> 00:21:48,159\nOK.\n\n402\n00:21:48,160 --> 00:21:49,160\nSo subtree of that.\n\n403\n00:21:49,160 --> 00:21:58,520\nSo where is it in this tree?\n\n404\n00:21:58,520 --> 00:21:59,020\nYeah.\n\n405\n00:21:59,020 --> 00:21:59,600\n.\n\n406\n00:21:59,600 --> 00:22:04,280\nNode can only be part of one subtree, right?\n\n407\n00:22:04,280 --> 00:22:05,920\nNode is actually part of many subtrees.\n\n408\n00:22:05,920 --> 00:22:07,280\nGood question.\n\n409\n00:22:07,280 --> 00:22:10,520\nf is in the subtree of f.\n\n410\n00:22:10,520 --> 00:22:12,160\nf is also in the subtree of d.\n\n411\n00:22:12,160 --> 00:22:14,200\nf is in the subtree of b, like I drew.\n\n412\n00:22:14,200 --> 00:22:15,400\nf is in the subtree of a.\n\n413\n00:22:15,400 --> 00:22:17,520\nIt's in the subtree of exactly its ancestors.\n\n414\n00:22:17,520 --> 00:22:18,020\nOK.\n\n415\n00:22:18,020 --> 00:22:19,020\nSo it's in the subtree of a.\n\n416\n00:22:19,020 --> 00:22:20,020\nSo it's in the subtree of a.\n\n417\n00:22:20,020 --> 00:22:21,020\nSo it's in the subtree of a.\n\n418\n00:22:21,020 --> 00:22:24,260\nBut in this operation, when we define node,\n\n419\n00:22:24,260 --> 00:22:26,260\nour node only defines one subtree.\n\n420\n00:22:26,260 --> 00:22:28,340\nIt is the root of only one subtree.\n\n421\n00:22:28,340 --> 00:22:30,920\nAnd that's the subtree we're talking about.\n\n422\n00:22:30,920 --> 00:22:33,180\nAnd then I want to know among all those nodes, which\n\n423\n00:22:33,180 --> 00:22:37,920\nincludes node itself and other things, which one comes first\n\n424\n00:22:37,920 --> 00:22:39,020\nin this traversal order?\n\n425\n00:22:39,020 --> 00:22:42,680\nThis is like practice with traversal orders.\n\n426\n00:22:42,680 --> 00:22:44,180\nSo where should I look for this node?\n\n427\n00:22:44,180 --> 00:22:44,680\n.\n\n428\n00:22:44,680 --> 00:22:45,180\n.\n\n429\n00:22:45,180 --> 00:22:45,680\n.\n\n430\n00:22:45,680 --> 00:22:45,980\n.\n\n431\n00:22:45,980 --> 00:22:46,480\n.\n\n432\n00:22:46,480 --> 00:22:46,780\n.\n\n433\n00:22:46,780 --> 00:22:47,080\n.\n\n434\n00:22:47,080 --> 00:22:47,379\n.\n\n435\n00:22:47,379 --> 00:22:47,680\n.\n\n436\n00:22:47,680 --> 00:22:47,780\n.\n\n437\n00:22:47,780 --> 00:22:47,879\n.\n\n438\n00:22:47,879 --> 00:22:47,980\n.\n\n439\n00:22:48,020 --> 00:22:48,480\n.\n\n440\n00:22:48,480 --> 00:22:48,980\n.\n\n441\n00:22:48,980 --> 00:22:49,480\n.\n\n442\n00:22:49,480 --> 00:22:49,980\n.\n\n443\n00:22:49,980 --> 00:22:50,480\n.\n\n444\n00:22:50,480 --> 00:22:50,980\n.\n\n445\n00:22:50,980 --> 00:22:52,260\nThe leftmost leaf.\n\n446\n00:22:52,260 --> 00:22:53,000\nYeah.\n\n447\n00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:54,560\nIn the picture, it's here.\n\n448\n00:22:54,560 --> 00:22:57,340\nBut pictures can be deceiving.\n\n449\n00:22:57,340 --> 00:23:01,540\nWe just want to go left as much as possible.\n\n450\n00:23:01,540 --> 00:23:07,680\nWhen I say go left, I mean this iteration node equals node.left.\n\n451\n00:23:07,680 --> 00:23:10,580\nIf you just look at our definition,\n\n452\n00:23:10,580 --> 00:23:13,340\nall the nodes in the left come before x\n\n453\n00:23:13,340 --> 00:23:14,580\nand all the nodes in the right.\n\n454\n00:23:14,580 --> 00:23:17,879\nSo it's got to be in the left subtree if there is one.\n\n455\n00:23:17,880 --> 00:23:21,500\nOf course, we can't do this forever.\n\n456\n00:23:21,500 --> 00:23:31,500\nSo let's say until we would fall off the tree, which means node\n\n457\n00:23:31,500 --> 00:23:32,120\nis none.\n\n458\n00:23:35,300 --> 00:23:39,940\nWe stop before that would happen.\n\n459\n00:23:39,940 --> 00:23:42,260\nSo this is like the directions like, oh, you\n\n460\n00:23:42,260 --> 00:23:44,080\nkeep driving until you see this door,\n\n461\n00:23:44,080 --> 00:23:45,840\nand it's the block right before that.\n\n462\n00:23:45,840 --> 00:23:47,740\nIt's like, well, that's not very helpful.\n\n463\n00:23:47,740 --> 00:23:50,120\nSo you keep iterating node equals node.left\n\n464\n00:23:50,120 --> 00:23:53,680\nuntil node becomes none, and then you undo one step.\n\n465\n00:23:53,680 --> 00:23:55,060\nYou all know how to program that.\n\n466\n00:23:55,060 --> 00:23:57,060\nIt's not hard.\n\n467\n00:23:57,060 --> 00:24:00,400\nSo that last non-none node, which might actually\n\n468\n00:24:00,400 --> 00:24:02,640\nbe the root, it might be node.\n\n469\n00:24:02,640 --> 00:24:04,920\nMaybe it has no left children.\n\n470\n00:24:04,920 --> 00:24:07,200\nBut in that case, I claim node is the very first\n\n471\n00:24:07,200 --> 00:24:10,180\nin its subtree traversal order because if there\n\n472\n00:24:10,180 --> 00:24:12,800\nare no nodes in the left that come before x,\n\n473\n00:24:12,800 --> 00:24:16,220\nthen x is actually first.\n\n474\n00:24:16,220 --> 00:24:17,080\nSo that's it.\n\n475\n00:24:17,080 --> 00:24:17,400\n.\n\n476\n00:24:17,400 --> 00:24:18,900\nReturn node.\n\n477\n00:24:22,680 --> 00:24:24,540\nSo I'm modifying node in place here.\n\n478\n00:24:24,540 --> 00:24:27,580\nAnd the very last one before I hit none, that's the minimum.\n\n479\n00:24:27,580 --> 00:24:30,240\nThat's the first item in the traversal order.\n\n480\n00:24:30,240 --> 00:24:33,260\nSimilarly, you can define subtree last.\n\n481\n00:24:33,260 --> 00:24:35,480\nLet's do a more interesting one.\n\n482\n00:24:35,480 --> 00:24:35,980\nSuccessor.\n\n483\n00:24:41,460 --> 00:24:43,180\nNode.\n\n484\n00:24:43,180 --> 00:24:46,200\nSo in this case, I want to know what is the next.\n\n485\n00:24:47,400 --> 00:24:56,920\nAfter node in the overall tree's traversal order.\n\n486\n00:24:56,920 --> 00:25:01,700\nHere, I was restricting to a single subtree.\n\n487\n00:25:01,700 --> 00:25:03,220\nNow I'm thinking about the entire tree\n\n488\n00:25:03,220 --> 00:25:04,460\nin the entire traversal order.\n\n489\n00:25:04,460 --> 00:25:08,060\nThen, given a node, I want to know which one comes next.\n\n490\n00:25:08,060 --> 00:25:11,180\nCall this the successor.\n\n491\n00:25:11,180 --> 00:25:15,080\nI feel like I should make some kind of royal family joke now,\n\n492\n00:25:15,080 --> 00:25:16,280\nbut I don't know how.\n\n493\n00:25:16,280 --> 00:25:16,700\nSo.\n\n494\n00:25:16,700 --> 00:25:20,740\nSo, every node has a unique successor.\n\n495\n00:25:20,740 --> 00:25:22,940\nLet's do some examples.\n\n496\n00:25:22,940 --> 00:25:28,980\nSo we can start with F. The successor of F, if we just index into this list, the successor\n\n497\n00:25:28,980 --> 00:25:31,760\nis D. Okay?\n\n498\n00:25:31,760 --> 00:25:36,500\nSuccessor of D is B. Successor of B is E. It's very easy to read successors off when\n\n499\n00:25:36,500 --> 00:25:38,380\nI have the traversal order written down.\n\n500\n00:25:38,380 --> 00:25:41,820\nBut let's think about how to do it in the tree.\n"}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_02_vyw6NWlgjlC1bsr25OuC1786","name":"read_file","content":"[range 2001-2500 of 4060 lines]\n501\n00:25:41,820 --> 00:25:45,400\nOkay?\n\n502\n00:25:45,400 --> 00:25:46,400\nLet's see.\n\n503\n00:25:46,400 --> 00:25:47,780\nThere will be two cases.\n\n504\n00:25:47,780 --> 00:25:54,220\nIf I look at the successor of A, it has a right child.\n\n505\n00:25:54,220 --> 00:25:58,840\nAnd in this case, the right child of A is the successor, but that's not always the case.\n\n506\n00:25:58,840 --> 00:26:01,240\nI don't have an example.\n\n507\n00:26:01,240 --> 00:26:10,900\nBut if I had another node here, let's call it G, the successor of A is actually G. Okay?\n\n508\n00:26:10,900 --> 00:26:15,260\nBecause all of these items come after A in the order.\n\n509\n00:26:15,260 --> 00:26:16,260\nBut which one comes first?\n\n510\n00:26:16,260 --> 00:26:18,120\nThe leftmost leaf.\n\n511\n00:26:18,120 --> 00:26:19,120\nOkay?\n\n512\n00:26:19,120 --> 00:26:21,020\nThat's the problem we just solved.\n\n513\n00:26:21,020 --> 00:26:26,800\nSo if A has a right child, what we want is the leftmost leaf, the first thing in that\n\n514\n00:26:26,800 --> 00:26:30,180\nsubtree.\n\n515\n00:26:30,180 --> 00:26:31,180\nThe right subtree.\n\n516\n00:26:31,180 --> 00:26:32,180\nRight child subtree.\n\n517\n00:26:32,180 --> 00:26:34,180\nSo this is case one.\n\n518\n00:26:34,180 --> 00:26:42,640\nIf node dot right, so if we have a right child, then what we want is subtree first.\n\n519\n00:26:42,640 --> 00:26:43,640\nRight child.\n\n520\n00:26:43,640 --> 00:26:44,640\nRight child.\n\n521\n00:26:44,640 --> 00:26:45,640\nRight child.\n\n522\n00:26:45,640 --> 00:26:51,620\nSo this is the first of the right child.\n\n523\n00:26:51,620 --> 00:26:55,260\nOkay, great.\n\n524\n00:26:55,260 --> 00:26:57,580\nWe can reduce to this other operation.\n\n525\n00:26:57,580 --> 00:27:03,800\nBut what if the node doesn't have a right child?\n\n526\n00:27:03,800 --> 00:27:05,640\nFor example, it could be a leaf.\n\n527\n00:27:05,640 --> 00:27:08,980\nSay we're taking the successor of, I mean, it doesn't have to be a leaf.\n\n528\n00:27:08,980 --> 00:27:10,460\nIt could be F which has no children.\n\n529\n00:27:10,460 --> 00:27:14,420\nIt could be D which has one child, but no right child.\n\n530\n00:27:14,420 --> 00:27:15,520\nSo what's the successor of F?\n\n531\n00:27:15,520 --> 00:27:19,760\nWell, it's D, which in this case is the parent,\n\n532\n00:27:19,760 --> 00:27:20,760\nbut it's not always.\n\n533\n00:27:20,760 --> 00:27:22,480\nFor example, if we do successor of E,\n\n534\n00:27:22,480 --> 00:27:24,960\nits parent is actually earlier in the order\n\n535\n00:27:24,960 --> 00:27:26,500\nbecause E was a right child.\n\n536\n00:27:26,500 --> 00:27:31,139\nHere, F was a left child, and so its parent was after.\n\n537\n00:27:33,900 --> 00:27:39,300\nSuccessor of D happens to be B because it was the left child\n\n538\n00:27:39,300 --> 00:27:40,280\nof its parent.\n\n539\n00:27:40,280 --> 00:27:41,820\nOK, so that seems like the easy case.\n\n540\n00:27:41,820 --> 00:27:43,360\nIf we're the left child of our parent,\n\n541\n00:27:43,360 --> 00:27:47,219\nthen our successor is our parent,\n\n542\n00:27:47,219 --> 00:27:48,919\nbasing on this small example.\n\n543\n00:27:48,919 --> 00:27:50,719\nBut we can argue it generally in a moment.\n\n544\n00:27:50,719 --> 00:27:52,800\nWhat's the successor of E?\n\n545\n00:27:52,800 --> 00:27:54,560\nWell, it's not B because that comes earlier.\n\n546\n00:27:54,560 --> 00:27:57,120\nIn fact, all the things in B's subtree\n\n547\n00:27:57,120 --> 00:28:04,020\ncome earlier or equal to E. So we have to keep going up.\n\n548\n00:28:04,020 --> 00:28:05,740\nAnd then it turns out the successor of E\n\n549\n00:28:05,740 --> 00:28:10,179\nis A because this subtree was the left child of A,\n\n550\n00:28:10,179 --> 00:28:12,860\nbecause B was a left child of A. So\n\n551\n00:28:12,860 --> 00:28:13,360\nthe general structure.\n\n552\n00:28:13,360 --> 00:28:17,679\nThe general strategy is walk up the tree\n\n553\n00:28:17,679 --> 00:28:23,439\nuntil we go up a traversal whose reverse direction would\n\n554\n00:28:23,439 --> 00:28:25,080\nbe left.\n\n555\n00:28:25,080 --> 00:28:29,639\nSo walk up the tree.\n\n556\n00:28:29,639 --> 00:28:32,919\nWhen I say walk up, I mean node equals node.parent iteration.\n\n557\n00:28:32,920 --> 00:28:47,260\nUntil we go up a left branch.\n\n558\n00:28:50,680 --> 00:28:54,840\nSo this would mean that node, before we do the change,\n\n559\n00:28:54,840 --> 00:28:57,620\nnode equals node.parent.left.\n\n560\n00:29:02,920 --> 00:29:04,640\nSo we can check that.\n\n561\n00:29:04,640 --> 00:29:06,040\nAnd then after we do that traversal,\n\n562\n00:29:06,040 --> 00:29:09,080\nthat parent is exactly the node we're looking for.\n\n563\n00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:15,360\nWhy is this true in general?\n\n564\n00:29:15,360 --> 00:29:17,980\nLet me draw a more generic picture.\n\n565\n00:29:17,980 --> 00:29:19,440\nSo we're starting at some node.\n\n566\n00:29:21,960 --> 00:29:25,600\nAnd let's say its parent is to the right,\n\n567\n00:29:25,600 --> 00:29:29,080\nso it comes later in the order, for a while.\n\n568\n00:29:29,080 --> 00:29:30,000\nSorry.\n\n569\n00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:30,800\nGet this backwards.\n\n570\n00:29:30,800 --> 00:29:31,800\nWe're doing successor.\n\n571\n00:29:32,920 --> 00:29:38,560\nSo it goes to the left for a while.\n\n572\n00:29:38,560 --> 00:29:40,960\nSo all these nodes will come earlier in the order,\n\n573\n00:29:40,960 --> 00:29:43,380\nbecause by the definition, everything in the right subtree\n\n574\n00:29:43,380 --> 00:29:44,200\ncomes after.\n\n575\n00:29:44,200 --> 00:29:46,580\nAnd at some point, we have a parent that's to the right,\n\n576\n00:29:46,580 --> 00:29:50,300\nmeaning this node was the left child of this parent.\n\n577\n00:29:50,300 --> 00:29:54,000\nAnd that node, by definition, will come after all of the nodes\n\n578\n00:29:54,000 --> 00:29:55,480\nin here.\n\n579\n00:29:55,480 --> 00:30:00,760\nAnd could there be anything in between node and this parent,\n\n580\n00:30:00,760 --> 00:30:02,080\ngrandparent, ancestor?\n\n581\n00:30:02,080 --> 00:30:02,520\nNo.\n\n582\n00:30:02,520 --> 00:30:05,700\nOnly if there was something in this subtree.\n\n583\n00:30:05,700 --> 00:30:07,600\nAnd we're in the case here where there\n\n584\n00:30:07,600 --> 00:30:10,400\nis no right subtree of our original node.\n\n585\n00:30:10,400 --> 00:30:13,400\nSo this is where all the nodes in between node and here\n\n586\n00:30:13,400 --> 00:30:15,060\nwould be, but there aren't any.\n\n587\n00:30:15,060 --> 00:30:17,920\nAnd therefore, this is the successor.\n\n588\n00:30:17,920 --> 00:30:21,960\nSo that's sort of the general argument why this works.\n\n589\n00:30:21,960 --> 00:30:22,820\nI see a question.\n\n590\n00:30:22,820 --> 00:30:23,320\nYeah.\n\n591\n00:30:23,320 --> 00:30:26,360\nSo would you say that if you go up a left branch,\n\n592\n00:30:26,360 --> 00:30:30,360\nthat means that you already placed the node of the subtree\n\n593\n00:30:30,360 --> 00:30:32,360\nthat is the left child into the left?\n\n594\n00:30:32,360 --> 00:30:37,179\nAnd then you could put a traversal order to it?\n\n595\n00:30:37,179 --> 00:30:38,520\nPlaced into the traversal order.\n\n596\n00:30:38,520 --> 00:30:42,780\nSo the traversal order is never explicitly computed.\n\n597\n00:30:42,780 --> 00:30:44,280\nIt's always implicit.\n\n598\n00:30:44,280 --> 00:30:48,780\nWe can't afford to maintain this as, say, an array.\n\n599\n00:30:48,780 --> 00:30:50,320\nThis is just in our heads.\n\n600\n00:30:50,320 --> 00:30:55,199\nMaybe I will draw it with a cloud around it.\n\n601\n00:30:55,199 --> 00:30:58,080\nWe're just thinking this.\n\n602\n00:30:58,080 --> 00:31:00,500\nIt's not in the computer explicitly.\n\n603\n00:31:00,500 --> 00:31:02,240\nIn the computer, all we store is this.\n\n604\n00:31:02,240 --> 00:31:05,200\nAnd the reason is, this is expensive.\n\n605\n00:31:05,200 --> 00:31:07,440\nWe can't maintain an array of things\n\n606\n00:31:07,440 --> 00:31:10,520\nand be able to insert it in the middle, whereas this is cheap.\n\n607\n00:31:10,520 --> 00:31:12,640\nI can afford to maintain this structure\n\n608\n00:31:12,640 --> 00:31:13,640\nand do all these things.\n\n609\n00:31:13,640 --> 00:31:15,740\nAnd so the reason we're talking about these operations\n\n610\n00:31:15,740 --> 00:31:19,100\nis they're letting us manipulate the order, or in this case,\n\n611\n00:31:19,100 --> 00:31:21,520\nletting us iterate through the order.\n\n612\n00:31:21,520 --> 00:31:23,060\nSo this was an algorithm for iterating\n\n613\n00:31:23,060 --> 00:31:26,760\nthrough the entire order, but that takes linear time.\n\n614\n00:31:26,760 --> 00:31:28,800\nThis was getting started in the order.\n\n615\n00:31:28,800 --> 00:31:31,580\nFind me the first thing in the order.\n\n616\n00:31:31,580 --> 00:31:32,080\nAnd this was.\n\n617\n00:31:32,080 --> 00:31:35,159\nGiven one node, find me the next one.\n\n618\n00:31:35,159 --> 00:31:36,939\nHow long did these operations take?\n\n619\n00:31:48,899 --> 00:31:49,399\nYeah?\n\n620\n00:31:49,399 --> 00:31:50,699\n.\n\n621\n00:31:56,480 --> 00:31:56,980\nRight.\n\n622\n00:31:56,980 --> 00:31:58,879\nAt most, the height of the entire tree.\n\n623\n00:31:58,879 --> 00:32:02,080\nIn fact, it's going to be the depth of that first node.\n\n624\n00:32:02,080 --> 00:32:05,159\nBut in the worst case, that's the height of the entire tree.\n\n625\n00:32:05,159 --> 00:32:06,720\nIn general, all of these operations\n"}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_03_TOPQnhg4rdUrbXxW0yWh4167","name":"read_file","content":"[range 2501-3000 of 4060 lines]\n626\n00:32:06,720 --> 00:32:09,020\nare going to be order h.\n\n627\n00:32:09,020 --> 00:32:11,520\nWe need to think about it in each case,\n\n628\n00:32:11,520 --> 00:32:13,820\nexcept for this one, which is order n.\n\n629\n00:32:13,820 --> 00:32:16,879\nSo we're iterating through the whole thing.\n\n630\n00:32:16,879 --> 00:32:18,899\nIn this case, we're just calling subtree first.\n\n631\n00:32:18,899 --> 00:32:20,740\nSo that takes order h time.\n\n632\n00:32:20,740 --> 00:32:22,699\nHere, we're walking up the tree instead of down,\n\n633\n00:32:22,699 --> 00:32:25,580\nbut that's going to cost exactly the height of the node.\n\n634\n00:32:25,580 --> 00:32:28,600\nWe happen to stop early, but worst case, order h.\n\n635\n00:32:28,600 --> 00:32:31,899\nFor all the operations we consider today, we just want to get an order.\n\n636\n00:32:31,900 --> 00:32:35,660\nWe get an order h bound, and later we will bound h.\n\n637\n00:32:35,660 --> 00:32:37,900\nSo the point is, these are fast.\n\n638\n00:32:37,900 --> 00:32:42,020\nIf h is small, like log n, these are almost instantaneous.\n\n639\n00:32:42,020 --> 00:32:45,780\nWhereas if I had to update the explicit traversal order, say,\n\n640\n00:32:45,780 --> 00:32:48,060\nas an array, I would have to spend linear time\n\n641\n00:32:48,060 --> 00:32:49,980\nevery time I make a change.\n\n642\n00:32:49,980 --> 00:32:52,700\nAnd yes, it would be fast to do successor\n\n643\n00:32:52,700 --> 00:32:55,640\nif I had this stored explicitly, but maintaining it\n\n644\n00:32:55,640 --> 00:32:56,620\nwould be impossible.\n\n645\n00:32:56,620 --> 00:32:58,420\nMaintaining it efficiently would be impossible.\n\n646\n00:32:58,420 --> 00:32:58,920\nQuestion?\n\n647\n00:33:01,900 --> 00:33:05,160\n.\n\n648\n00:33:05,160 --> 00:33:05,660\nYes.\n\n649\n00:33:14,040 --> 00:33:15,720\nCool.\n\n650\n00:33:15,720 --> 00:33:18,400\nSo these were queries where I want\n\n651\n00:33:18,400 --> 00:33:24,380\nto see what's next in the traversal sequence.\n\n652\n00:33:24,380 --> 00:33:26,920\nNow let's talk about actually changing the traversal sequence.\n\n653\n00:33:26,920 --> 00:33:29,120\nSo these are insert and delete operations.\n\n654\n00:33:29,120 --> 00:33:31,660\nThese will correspond roughly to insert\n\n655\n00:33:31,660 --> 00:33:36,900\nat or delete at, but we're not quite in the sequence world yet.\n\n656\n00:33:36,900 --> 00:33:39,620\nInstead, we're going to focus on inserting or deleting\n\n657\n00:33:39,620 --> 00:33:41,160\nin the middle of a subtree.\n\n658\n00:33:47,640 --> 00:33:50,100\nSo I'm going to have two nodes.\n\n659\n00:33:56,340 --> 00:33:56,840\nSo the.\n\n660\n00:33:56,840 --> 00:33:57,720\n.\n\n661\n00:33:57,720 --> 00:33:58,920\n.\n\n662\n00:33:58,920 --> 00:33:59,700\n.\n\n663\n00:33:59,700 --> 00:34:00,780\n.\n\n664\n00:34:00,780 --> 00:34:00,920\n.\n\n665\n00:34:00,920 --> 00:34:08,240\n.\n\n666\n00:34:08,240 --> 00:34:12,420\nThe first between inside or back,\n\n667\n00:34:12,420 --> 00:34:14,300\nand then what I can do with these lenses\n\n668\n00:34:14,300 --> 00:34:17,340\nis extend the number of contest data\n\n669\n00:34:17,340 --> 00:34:20,100\nafter setzen are independent.\n\n670\n00:34:20,100 --> 00:34:22,360\nSo what are the load parameters that are going\n\n671\n00:34:22,360 --> 00:34:25,100\nto be working on thoseensen, n the set of index passes Syrian\n\n672\n00:34:25,100 --> 00:34:26,380\nidentity probabilities.\n\n673\n00:34:26,380 --> 00:34:28,280\nSo that we're trying to further 好.\n\n674\n00:34:28,280 --> 00:34:29,420\nirresponsible by size.\n\n675\n00:34:29,420 --> 00:34:29,860\nThat.\n\n676\n00:34:29,860 --> 00:34:30,020\n.\n\n677\n00:34:30,020 --> 00:34:30,520\nbubble.\n\n678\n00:34:33,460 --> 00:34:35,300\nThat's what we want to achieve.\n\n679\n00:34:35,300 --> 00:34:38,400\nAnd we have to do it by manipulating this tree.\n\n680\n00:34:38,400 --> 00:34:40,040\nAnd however we change the tree, it\n\n681\n00:34:40,040 --> 00:34:43,020\ndefines a new traversal order.\n\n682\n00:34:43,020 --> 00:34:44,840\nSo maybe let's do an example first.\n\n683\n00:35:00,020 --> 00:35:06,560\nActually, I probably want this traversal order.\n\n684\n00:35:06,560 --> 00:35:08,620\nKeep track of that.\n\n685\n00:35:08,620 --> 00:35:23,080\nSo let's say the first thing we want to do is insert g before e.\n\n686\n00:35:23,080 --> 00:35:27,540\nI want to illustrate both of the operations.\n\n687\n00:35:27,540 --> 00:35:29,700\nInsert h.\n\n688\n00:35:30,020 --> 00:35:32,520\nAfter e.\n\n689\n00:35:32,520 --> 00:35:33,020\na.\n\n690\n00:35:41,440 --> 00:35:44,100\nSo insert g before e.\n\n691\n00:35:44,100 --> 00:35:49,200\nSo conceptually, what we want to do is insert g here.\n\n692\n00:35:49,200 --> 00:35:51,360\nSo we're given the node e.\n\n693\n00:35:51,360 --> 00:35:53,580\nAnd we're given a sort of empty node,\n\n694\n00:35:53,580 --> 00:35:55,120\nI mean a node that just contains g.\n\n695\n00:35:55,120 --> 00:35:56,920\nIt doesn't have any pointers yet.\n\n696\n00:35:56,920 --> 00:35:59,840\nAnd we would like to put it before e.\n\n697\n00:35:59,840 --> 00:36:00,020\nWhere should we put it?\n\n698\n00:36:00,020 --> 00:36:02,259\nWhere should I put it?\n\n699\n00:36:02,259 --> 00:36:04,980\nLeft child.\n\n700\n00:36:04,980 --> 00:36:07,259\nSo this is an easy case.\n\n701\n00:36:07,259 --> 00:36:10,020\nIf I'm trying to insert before and there's no left child,\n\n702\n00:36:10,020 --> 00:36:10,680\nstick it there.\n\n703\n00:36:10,680 --> 00:36:13,460\nIf I'm trying to insert after and there's no right child,\n\n704\n00:36:13,460 --> 00:36:14,259\nstick it there.\n\n705\n00:36:14,259 --> 00:36:15,200\nEasy.\n\n706\n00:36:15,200 --> 00:36:16,580\nSo let me write down case one.\n\n707\n00:36:20,800 --> 00:36:22,640\nSo here we're inserting after.\n\n708\n00:36:22,640 --> 00:36:26,840\nSo if there's no right child.\n\n709\n00:36:30,020 --> 00:36:39,780\nSo I'm going to put new there.\n\n710\n00:36:39,780 --> 00:36:40,280\nOK.\n\n711\n00:36:40,280 --> 00:36:45,259\nI'm using informal language here, putting this new node there.\n\n712\n00:36:45,259 --> 00:36:50,020\nInstead of writing, for example, node dot write equals new.\n\n713\n00:36:50,020 --> 00:36:52,640\nBecause that's only one operation you need to do.\n\n714\n00:36:52,640 --> 00:36:55,020\nOne thing you would do is set node dot write equals to new.\n\n715\n00:36:55,020 --> 00:36:58,020\nBut you also have to set new's parent to be node dot write.\n\n716\n00:36:58,020 --> 00:36:59,320\nSo instead of worrying about those two pointer changes, because we always do bidirectional,\n\n717\n00:36:59,320 --> 00:36:59,860\nwe're going to do set node dot write equals to new.\n\n718\n00:36:59,860 --> 00:37:00,000\nSo instead of worrying about those two pointer changes, because we always do bidirectional,\n\n719\n00:37:00,020 --> 00:37:02,920\npointer changes, I'm just going to use pseudocode.\n\n720\n00:37:02,920 --> 00:37:07,920\nAnd then in recitation, you'll see actual Python code that does all this.\n\n721\n00:37:07,920 --> 00:37:12,580\nSo then there's the other case.\n\n722\n00:37:12,580 --> 00:37:14,840\nSo that should be the second example.\n\n723\n00:37:14,840 --> 00:37:17,200\nInsert h after a.\n\n724\n00:37:20,660 --> 00:37:21,680\nInsert h after a.\n\n725\n00:37:21,680 --> 00:37:26,580\nSo we already have a node after a in this right subtree.\n\n726\n00:37:29,380 --> 00:37:29,860\nSo where do I write?\n\n727\n00:37:29,860 --> 00:37:35,960\nWhere do I want to put h relative to a?\n\n728\n00:37:35,960 --> 00:37:38,960\nWell, it should be to the right of a.\n\n729\n00:37:38,960 --> 00:37:41,360\nBut it should be before c.\n\n730\n00:37:41,360 --> 00:37:43,540\nIt should be to the left of c.\n\n731\n00:37:43,540 --> 00:37:47,300\nSo that would mean we want to put it here.\n\n732\n00:37:47,300 --> 00:37:50,500\nIn this case, it was pretty easy because this tree was small.\n\n733\n00:37:50,500 --> 00:37:52,220\nWhere do I want to put it in general?\n\n734\n00:37:52,220 --> 00:37:58,180\nWell, wherever subtree first tells me to put it.\n\n735\n00:37:58,180 --> 00:37:59,220\nSubtree first is going to give me, I'm going to put it in here.\n\n736\n00:37:59,220 --> 00:38:01,220\nIt's going to give me the successor.\n\n737\n00:38:01,220 --> 00:38:02,439\nThese are all kind of parallel.\n\n738\n00:38:05,319 --> 00:38:09,060\nWe're in the case now where our node has a right child.\n\n739\n00:38:09,060 --> 00:38:11,540\nAnd then successor tells us where the successor is.\n\n740\n00:38:11,540 --> 00:38:16,100\nIt is the first node, which is the leftmost descendant,\n\n741\n00:38:16,100 --> 00:38:19,500\nin the right subtree of the node.\n\n742\n00:38:19,500 --> 00:38:21,480\nA lot of pointers to follow in that sentence,\n\n743\n00:38:21,480 --> 00:38:26,020\nbut it's clear in the picture.\n\n744\n00:38:26,020 --> 00:38:29,000\nSo in this case, we had node, and there was no right child.\n\n745\n00:38:29,220 --> 00:38:33,959\nSo we just added new to be its right child.\n\n746\n00:38:33,959 --> 00:38:37,520\nIn the other case, we had a right child.\n\n747\n00:38:37,520 --> 00:38:39,080\nSo here is node.\n\n748\n00:38:42,379 --> 00:38:45,200\nThere's this node here, node.right,\n\n749\n00:38:45,200 --> 00:38:46,560\nwhich now we're supposing exists.\n\n750\n00:38:49,220 --> 00:38:50,939\nAnd it defines a whole subtree.\n"}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_04_moC27Z2lwRSeQ5RmMDTk3020","name":"read_file","content":"[range 3001-3500 of 4060 lines]\n751\n00:38:50,939 --> 00:38:56,299\nThere's this one, which is the first node\n\n752\n00:38:56,299 --> 00:38:58,319\nin the traversal order of the subtree, also known as the successor of it.\n\n753\n00:38:58,319 --> 00:38:58,339\nOK.\n\n754\n00:38:58,339 --> 00:38:58,439\nOK.\n\n755\n00:38:58,439 --> 00:38:58,500\nOK.\n\n756\n00:38:58,500 --> 00:38:58,540\nOK.\n\n757\n00:38:58,540 --> 00:38:58,620\nOK.\n\n758\n00:38:58,620 --> 00:38:58,680\nOK.\n\n759\n00:38:58,680 --> 00:38:58,779\nOK.\n\n760\n00:38:58,779 --> 00:38:58,839\nOK.\n\n761\n00:38:58,839 --> 00:38:58,879\nOK.\n\n762\n00:38:58,879 --> 00:38:59,020\nOK.\n\n763\n00:38:59,020 --> 00:38:59,040\nOK.\n\n764\n00:38:59,040 --> 00:38:59,080\nOK.\n\n765\n00:38:59,080 --> 00:38:59,140\nOK.\n\n766\n00:38:59,140 --> 00:38:59,220\nOK.\n\n767\n00:38:59,220 --> 00:38:59,720\nOK.\n\n768\n00:38:59,720 --> 00:39:07,240\nSo it's the successor of node.\n\n769\n00:39:07,240 --> 00:39:12,480\nI'll call this successor of node, in the current traversal order.\n\n770\n00:39:12,480 --> 00:39:17,339\nBut, of course, we'd like to make new the new successor of the node.\n\n771\n00:39:17,339 --> 00:39:19,319\nSo where does it go?\n\n772\n00:39:19,319 --> 00:39:19,839\nHere.\n\n773\n00:39:19,839 --> 00:39:23,959\nWe want to add it as a left child to the old successor.\n\n774\n00:39:23,959 --> 00:39:24,540\nOK.\n\n775\n00:39:24,540 --> 00:39:29,220\nSo put node, right?\n\n776\n00:39:29,220 --> 00:39:29,720\nas a.\n\n777\n00:39:40,459 --> 00:39:41,520\nSo take the successor.\n\n778\n00:39:41,520 --> 00:39:44,279\nAnd if you look at the code for successor, we're in this case.\n\n779\n00:39:44,279 --> 00:39:48,279\nSo we know it will just call subtree first of node.right.\n\n780\n00:39:48,279 --> 00:39:52,180\nAnd remember, subtree first went left as much as it possibly\n\n781\n00:39:52,180 --> 00:39:53,020\ncould.\n\n782\n00:39:53,020 --> 00:39:54,799\nSo what that means is this successor node\n\n783\n00:39:54,799 --> 00:39:58,399\nis guaranteed to not have a left child,\n\n784\n00:39:58,400 --> 00:40:01,080\nbecause it was defined by going right once and then going left\n\n785\n00:40:01,080 --> 00:40:02,660\nas much as you could.\n\n786\n00:40:02,660 --> 00:40:05,960\nSo there's no more left, which means we can make one more left.\n\n787\n00:40:05,960 --> 00:40:09,420\nJust add new there, and we're done.\n\n788\n00:40:09,420 --> 00:40:11,340\nNow if you look at the traversal order,\n\n789\n00:40:11,340 --> 00:40:14,080\nit will be node, then new, then the old successor,\n\n790\n00:40:14,080 --> 00:40:16,660\nand then the rest of that subtree.\n\n791\n00:40:16,660 --> 00:40:17,960\nIt's kind of cool.\n\n792\n00:40:17,960 --> 00:40:22,920\nIn all cases, this was constant time.\n\n793\n00:40:22,920 --> 00:40:25,680\nHere we spent constant time after we called successor.\n\n794\n00:40:25,680 --> 00:40:27,720\nSuccessor costs order h time.\n\n795\n00:40:27,720 --> 00:40:28,400\nSo this is order h.\n\n796\n00:40:28,400 --> 00:40:32,580\nNew or node?\n\n797\n00:40:32,580 --> 00:40:33,080\nHm?\n\n798\n00:40:33,080 --> 00:40:34,340\nNew or node?\n\n799\n00:40:34,340 --> 00:40:34,840\nNew.\n\n800\n00:40:34,840 --> 00:40:36,340\nNew.\n\n801\n00:40:36,340 --> 00:40:38,740\nOK.\n\n802\n00:40:38,740 --> 00:40:39,620\nPut new there.\n\n803\n00:40:44,800 --> 00:40:45,900\nClear?\n\n804\n00:40:45,900 --> 00:40:47,780\nOK, that was insertion.\n\n805\n00:40:47,780 --> 00:40:48,920\nLet's do deletion.\n\n806\n00:40:54,540 --> 00:40:58,080\nGet the spec right and the example.\n\n807\n00:40:58,400 --> 00:41:13,160\nAll of these are going to have two cases.\n\n808\n00:41:13,160 --> 00:41:16,700\nSo let me update.\n\n809\n00:41:16,700 --> 00:41:19,160\nSo now h is after a.\n\n810\n00:41:19,160 --> 00:41:20,400\nSo it should be like this.\n\n811\n00:41:20,400 --> 00:41:22,560\nAnd you can check the new traversal order of this tree\n\n812\n00:41:22,560 --> 00:41:23,520\nis exactly that.\n\n813\n00:41:26,500 --> 00:41:28,340\nNext, I'm going to do a couple of deletions.\n\n814\n00:41:28,400 --> 00:41:36,300\nLet's delete f first.\n\n815\n00:41:36,300 --> 00:41:42,900\nAnd then we're going to, well, this is confusing.\n\n816\n00:41:47,900 --> 00:41:49,720\nAnd then we're going to delete a.\n\n817\n00:41:49,720 --> 00:41:52,200\nSo where's f?\n\n818\n00:41:52,200 --> 00:41:55,400\nWe're supposing we're given a pointer to f, this node.\n\n819\n00:41:55,400 --> 00:41:56,280\nWell, it's a leaf.\n\n820\n00:41:56,280 --> 00:41:58,280\nSo if I want to delete it, I just\n\n821\n00:41:58,280 --> 00:42:01,460\nerase it, easy.\n\n822\n00:42:01,460 --> 00:42:02,580\nLeaves are easy to delete.\n\n823\n00:42:02,580 --> 00:42:03,980\nThere's no work to do.\n\n824\n00:42:03,980 --> 00:42:06,720\nSo what that means is I'm removing the pointer from d\n\n825\n00:42:06,720 --> 00:42:08,160\nto f.\n\n826\n00:42:08,160 --> 00:42:11,580\nWe just erase that guy.\n\n827\n00:42:11,580 --> 00:42:12,660\nNow here's a trickier one.\n\n828\n00:42:12,660 --> 00:42:14,660\nSuppose I want to delete the root of the tree.\n\n829\n00:42:14,660 --> 00:42:16,740\nThis is kind of the hardest case.\n\n830\n00:42:16,740 --> 00:42:18,260\nBut in general, it would be somewhere\n\n831\n00:42:18,260 --> 00:42:20,480\nin between leaf and root.\n\n832\n00:42:20,480 --> 00:42:23,560\nSo if I want to delete a, if I just erased it,\n\n833\n00:42:23,560 --> 00:42:25,800\nthen suddenly there are these pointers to nowhere.\n\n834\n00:42:25,800 --> 00:42:27,580\nAnd I disconnect the tree into two parts.\n\n835\n00:42:27,580 --> 00:42:28,500\nI don't want to do that.\n\n836\n00:42:28,500 --> 00:42:30,340\nI need to keep my tree connected.\n\n837\n00:42:30,340 --> 00:42:35,580\nSo I'm going to play this trick, which is,\n\n838\n00:42:35,580 --> 00:42:38,779\nI forget if I use successor or predecessor.\n\n839\n00:42:38,779 --> 00:42:39,279\nPredecessor.\n\n840\n00:42:43,660 --> 00:42:47,200\nSo I'm going to look at a.\n\n841\n00:42:47,200 --> 00:42:49,819\nWe already have defined successor and thereby\n\n842\n00:42:49,819 --> 00:42:51,319\npredecessor.\n\n843\n00:42:51,319 --> 00:42:56,259\nSo I'm going to look at the predecessor of a, which is e.\n\n844\n00:42:56,259 --> 00:42:57,420\nCan check that here.\n\n845\n00:42:57,420 --> 00:42:59,260\nWe have one before a is e.\n\n846\n00:42:59,260 --> 00:43:01,660\nThis is in the left subtree.\n\n847\n00:43:01,660 --> 00:43:03,280\nFind me the rightmost item.\n\n848\n00:43:03,280 --> 00:43:04,500\nKeep going right until I can't.\n\n849\n00:43:04,500 --> 00:43:05,940\nThat's e.\n\n850\n00:43:05,940 --> 00:43:07,780\nSo now these guys are adjacent in the order.\n\n851\n00:43:07,780 --> 00:43:09,980\nAnd I'm about to remove a from the order.\n\n852\n00:43:09,980 --> 00:43:14,080\nSo I can momentarily cheat and swap their labels.\n\n853\n00:43:14,080 --> 00:43:19,960\nI'm going to erase a and e here and put e after a.\n\n854\n00:43:19,960 --> 00:43:20,460\nWhy?\n\n855\n00:43:20,460 --> 00:43:22,960\nBecause it moves a down in the tree.\n\n856\n00:43:22,960 --> 00:43:24,940\nAnd if I get to the leaf, I'm done.\n\n857\n00:43:24,940 --> 00:43:26,800\nSo I'm not quite done because this is not a leaf.\n\n858\n00:43:26,800 --> 00:43:27,300\nSo again.\n\n859\n00:43:27,300 --> 00:43:28,500\nI look at a's predecessor.\n\n860\n00:43:28,500 --> 00:43:29,720\nIt's now g.\n\n861\n00:43:29,720 --> 00:43:37,100\nPredecessor, we hope, is always farther down in the tree.\n\n862\n00:43:37,100 --> 00:43:38,940\nAnd then I swap a with g.\n\n863\n00:43:42,820 --> 00:43:46,980\nI have preserved the traversal order except where a falls,\n\n864\n00:43:46,980 --> 00:43:49,500\njust by moving a earlier in the order here.\n\n865\n00:43:49,500 --> 00:43:51,840\nAnd now a is a leaf, and I can erase it.\n\n866\n00:43:54,720 --> 00:43:56,700\nSo that's what we're going to follow.\n\n867\n00:43:56,700 --> 00:43:57,200\nNow.\n\n868\n00:43:57,300 --> 00:43:58,680\nActuality, it's a little tricky.\n\n869\n00:43:58,680 --> 00:44:00,380\nSometimes we need to use predecessors.\n\n870\n00:44:00,380 --> 00:44:04,540\nSometimes we need to use successor.\n\n871\n00:44:04,540 --> 00:44:08,700\nSo the cases are if the node is a leaf,\n\n872\n00:44:08,700 --> 00:44:11,760\njust detach it from the parent.\n\n873\n00:44:11,760 --> 00:44:14,260\nEasy.\n\n874\n00:44:14,260 --> 00:44:16,560\nThat's sort of our base case in the recursion.\n\n875\n00:44:16,560 --> 00:44:22,220\nOtherwise, there are two cases.\n"}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_05_ubXESxDVdp4qLb289AP93977","name":"read_file","content":"[range 3501-4060 of 4060 lines]\n876\n00:44:22,220 --> 00:44:26,140\nSo if we're not a leaf, that means we have a left child\n\n877\n00:44:26,140 --> 00:44:27,300\nor a right child or both.\n\n878\n00:44:27,300 --> 00:44:29,760\nBoth is going to be the easy case.\n\n879\n00:44:29,760 --> 00:44:33,620\nBut in general, I have either there's a left child\n\n880\n00:44:33,620 --> 00:44:36,080\nor there's a right child.\n\n881\n00:44:36,080 --> 00:44:37,800\nIn either case, I'm going to be happy.\n\n882\n00:44:37,800 --> 00:44:39,120\nSo I don't need a both case.\n\n883\n00:44:41,940 --> 00:44:43,680\nSo what do I do?\n\n884\n00:44:43,680 --> 00:44:47,160\nIf I have a left child, that guarantees to me\n\n885\n00:44:47,160 --> 00:44:50,540\nthat the node's predecessor is inside that left subtree, which\n\n886\n00:44:50,540 --> 00:44:52,900\nmeans it's lower in the tree.\n\n887\n00:44:52,900 --> 00:44:54,900\nIf I didn't have a left child, the predecessor\n\n888\n00:44:54,900 --> 00:44:56,420\nwould actually be higher in the tree.\n\n889\n00:44:56,420 --> 00:44:57,300\nAnd I don't want to go higher.\n\n890\n00:44:57,300 --> 00:45:01,720\nSo if I have a left child, I know the predecessor is lower.\n\n891\n00:45:01,720 --> 00:45:08,960\nAnd so I'm going to swap my item, the contents of my node,\n\n892\n00:45:08,960 --> 00:45:11,300\nwith my predecessor's item.\n\n893\n00:45:19,220 --> 00:45:23,780\nAnd then I'm going to recursively delete predecessor.\n\n894\n00:45:27,300 --> 00:45:34,520\nAnd that's the case that we looked at in this code,\n\n895\n00:45:34,520 --> 00:45:36,960\nin this example, because we always had a left child.\n\n896\n00:45:36,960 --> 00:45:38,840\nIf we have a right child but no left child,\n\n897\n00:45:38,840 --> 00:45:39,800\nwe just do the reverse.\n\n898\n00:45:39,800 --> 00:45:41,980\nWe swap with our successor's item\n\n899\n00:45:41,980 --> 00:45:43,760\nand then delete the successor.\n\n900\n00:45:43,760 --> 00:45:45,620\nIn either case, we're going down.\n\n901\n00:45:45,620 --> 00:45:48,480\nAnd so if we start at some node, like the root,\n\n902\n00:45:48,480 --> 00:45:52,420\nevery time we do this operation, we're walking down.\n\n903\n00:45:52,420 --> 00:45:53,860\nAnd then we're walking down.\n\n904\n00:45:53,860 --> 00:45:55,600\nAnd in general, we'll keep walking down,\n\n905\n00:45:55,600 --> 00:45:57,140\nresuming where we left off, which means, oh, we're going to go down.\n\n906\n00:45:57,140 --> 00:45:59,200\nAnd that means total amount of time we spend\n\n907\n00:45:59,200 --> 00:46:03,140\nis proportional to the height of the tree, in the worst case.\n\n908\n00:46:03,140 --> 00:46:03,640\nQuestion?\n\n909\n00:46:03,640 --> 00:46:05,640\nWhy isn't the ?\n\n910\n00:46:05,640 --> 00:46:10,640\nBecause we end up with like, now he has a right child.\n\n911\n00:46:10,640 --> 00:46:15,140\nYou see, but he never had a right child.\n\n912\n00:46:15,140 --> 00:46:16,140\nJOHN MCCUTCHAN- Right.\n\n913\n00:46:16,140 --> 00:46:17,759\nSo he didn't used to have a right child.\n\n914\n00:46:17,759 --> 00:46:19,359\nSo we're changing identities of nodes\n\n915\n00:46:19,359 --> 00:46:23,080\nwhen we do this, because we didn't actually\n\n916\n00:46:23,080 --> 00:46:24,259\nmove this circle.\n\n917\n00:46:24,259 --> 00:46:25,420\nThe circle stayed in place.\n\n918\n00:46:25,420 --> 00:46:26,960\nAnd what we changed was the item that\n\n919\n00:46:26,960 --> 00:46:28,720\nwas stored in that circle.\n\n920\n00:46:28,720 --> 00:46:33,200\nSo whether you call this node E or A, it doesn't really matter.\n\n921\n00:46:33,200 --> 00:46:35,820\nIt is just the root node.\n\n922\n00:46:35,820 --> 00:46:37,560\nSo we're going to play a lot of these tricks\n\n923\n00:46:37,560 --> 00:46:38,700\nof moving the items around.\n\n924\n00:46:38,700 --> 00:46:40,100\nSo far, we hadn't been doing that.\n\n925\n00:46:40,100 --> 00:46:43,880\nWe've just been creating nodes and placing them somewhere.\n\n926\n00:46:43,880 --> 00:46:45,740\nBut now, in this delete operation,\n\n927\n00:46:45,740 --> 00:46:47,540\nthis is the first time where we're changing\n\n928\n00:46:47,540 --> 00:46:49,400\nwhat's stored in the nodes.\n\n929\n00:46:49,400 --> 00:46:51,340\nBut we still can define the traversal order.\n\n930\n00:46:51,340 --> 00:46:55,780\nThe traversal order of this tree is D, B, G, E, H, C, which\n\n931\n00:46:55,780 --> 00:46:56,960\nshould be what we get here.\n\n932\n00:46:56,960 --> 00:46:59,960\nIf I delete F and A.\n\n933\n00:46:59,960 --> 00:47:02,960\nWhat if you created a local tree\n\n934\n00:47:02,960 --> 00:47:04,720\nand you wanted to make it different?\n\n935\n00:47:04,720 --> 00:47:05,520\nSorry, F.\n\n936\n00:47:05,520 --> 00:47:07,600\nLike, it really matters to you.\n\n937\n00:47:07,600 --> 00:47:09,520\nLike, it really matters to me, so you're\n\n938\n00:47:09,520 --> 00:47:11,460\ngoing to connect it that way.\n\n939\n00:47:11,460 --> 00:47:13,620\nCHRISTOPHER COTTEN- Trees will not preserve connections.\n\n940\n00:47:13,620 --> 00:47:15,120\nThat's just the name of the game.\n\n941\n00:47:15,120 --> 00:47:16,700\nWe have to allow this.\n\n942\n00:47:16,700 --> 00:47:18,100\nOtherwise, we can't do anything.\n\n943\n00:47:18,100 --> 00:47:19,760\nThat's the short version.\n\n944\n00:47:19,760 --> 00:47:21,200\nOK?\n\n945\n00:47:21,200 --> 00:47:21,700\nOK.\n\n946\n00:47:21,700 --> 00:47:24,720\nIn the last few minutes, let me talk about how we take these\n\n947\n00:47:24,720 --> 00:47:26,820\ntrees and implement a solution.\n\n948\n00:47:26,820 --> 00:47:28,880\nA set or sequence.\n\n949\n00:47:28,880 --> 00:47:29,380\nOK?\n\n950\n00:47:29,380 --> 00:47:30,680\nI've already alluded to this.\n\n951\n00:47:34,980 --> 00:47:44,800\nSo for a sequence, we just make the traversal order\n\n952\n00:47:44,800 --> 00:47:49,420\nequal to the order that we're trying to represent,\n\n953\n00:47:49,420 --> 00:47:50,260\nthe sequence order.\n\n954\n00:47:53,700 --> 00:47:56,380\nAnd if we're trying to sort a set of items with keys,\n\n955\n00:47:56,380 --> 00:48:02,300\nwe're going to make the traversal order equal to,\n\n956\n00:48:02,300 --> 00:48:05,000\nordered by increasing key.\n\n957\n00:48:05,000 --> 00:48:08,820\nIncreasing item key.\n\n958\n00:48:13,320 --> 00:48:14,820\nIn some sense, that's it.\n\n959\n00:48:14,820 --> 00:48:17,320\nBut then we need to think about how do we implement\n\n960\n00:48:17,320 --> 00:48:19,060\nall of these operations.\n\n961\n00:48:19,060 --> 00:48:23,260\nSo maybe most enlightening is, for starters,\n\n962\n00:48:23,260 --> 00:48:26,300\nis finding a key in a tree.\n\n963\n00:48:26,380 --> 00:48:31,740\nSo this is going to correspond to binary search.\n\n964\n00:48:31,740 --> 00:48:36,600\nIf I'm searching for a key, let's say I'm searching for G's\n\n965\n00:48:36,600 --> 00:48:41,000\nkey, and I know, this may be hard in this example,\n\n966\n00:48:41,000 --> 00:48:43,840\nmaybe I'll replace these all with numbers\n\n967\n00:48:43,840 --> 00:48:48,820\nso I can think about key values.\n\n968\n00:48:48,820 --> 00:48:49,320\nOK.\n\n969\n00:48:49,320 --> 00:48:54,540\nSo let's say 1, 7, 12, 17, 19, and 25.\n\n970\n00:48:54,540 --> 00:48:55,040\nOK.\n\n971\n00:48:55,040 --> 00:48:55,540\nSo I'm going to do this.\n\n972\n00:48:55,540 --> 00:48:56,060\nI'm going to do this.\n\n973\n00:48:56,060 --> 00:48:58,560\n1, 7, 12, 17, 19, and 25.\n\n974\n00:48:58,560 --> 00:49:01,000\nThis is now in key order.\n\n975\n00:49:01,000 --> 00:49:02,500\nIf you think of the traversal order,\n\n976\n00:49:02,500 --> 00:49:04,799\nthe property is that all the keys\n\n977\n00:49:04,799 --> 00:49:07,500\nin the left subtree of the root are less than the root,\n\n978\n00:49:07,500 --> 00:49:10,000\nand the root is less than all the keys in the right subtree,\n\n979\n00:49:10,000 --> 00:49:11,779\nand recursively all the way down.\n\n980\n00:49:11,779 --> 00:49:17,680\nThis is something called the binary search tree property,\n\n981\n00:49:17,680 --> 00:49:20,080\nBST property.\n\n982\n00:49:20,080 --> 00:49:23,540\nHere we're calling them binary tree sets,\n\n983\n00:49:23,540 --> 00:49:25,220\nor set binary trees.\n\n984\n00:49:25,220 --> 00:49:26,879\nBut they're also known in the literature\n\n985\n00:49:26,879 --> 00:49:29,500\nas binary search trees, a term you may have heard before.\n\n986\n00:49:29,500 --> 00:49:30,859\nSo this is a special case of what\n\n987\n00:49:30,859 --> 00:49:33,600\nwe're doing where we're storing the keys in order.\n\n988\n00:49:33,600 --> 00:49:37,919\nAnd then if I want to search for a key like 13,\n\n989\n00:49:37,919 --> 00:49:39,819\nI compare that key with the root.\n\n990\n00:49:39,819 --> 00:49:43,100\nI see, oh, it's not equal, and it's to the left\n\n991\n00:49:43,100 --> 00:49:44,799\nbecause it's less than 17.\n\n992\n00:49:44,799 --> 00:49:46,919\nSo 13 is left of here.\n\n993\n00:49:46,919 --> 00:49:48,700\n13 is right of 7.\n\n994\n00:49:48,700 --> 00:49:50,359\n13 is right of 12.\n\n995\n00:49:50,359 --> 00:49:55,060\nAnd so I know that this is where 13 would belong, but there's no\n\n996\n00:49:55,060 --> 00:49:55,740\nright child there.\n\n997\n00:49:55,740 --> 00:49:58,740\nAnd so I know in find, I just return nothing.\n\n998\n00:49:58,740 --> 00:50:03,640\nIf I was doing find previous, I would return this node\n\n999\n00:50:03,640 --> 00:50:05,920\nbecause I have tried to go to the right.\n\n1000\n00:50:05,920 --> 00:50:08,360\nThe last time before I fell off the tree,\n\n1001\n00:50:08,360 --> 00:50:09,740\nI was trying to go to the right.\n\n1002\n00:50:09,740 --> 00:50:12,860\nAnd therefore, that last node I had was the previous item.\n\n1003\n00:50:12,860 --> 00:50:15,340\nIf I was trying to do find next, what would I do?\n\n1004\n00:50:15,340 --> 00:50:18,600\nI would just take this node and compute its successor, which\n\n1005\n00:50:18,600 --> 00:50:19,860\nwe already know how to do.\n\n1006\n00:50:19,860 --> 00:50:22,120\nAnd that happens to be the root.\n\n1007\n00:50:22,120 --> 00:50:24,640\nSo now I can do these inexact searches.\n\n1008\n00:50:24,640 --> 00:50:27,940\nWhen I do find previous and find next, when I fall off the tree,\n\n1009\n00:50:27,940 --> 00:50:30,020\nI find either the previous or the next.\n\n1010\n00:50:30,020 --> 00:50:31,680\nAnd then with predecessor or successor,\n\n1011\n00:50:31,680 --> 00:50:33,420\nI can find the other one.\n\n1012\n00:50:33,420 --> 00:50:41,100\nSo that's how we can do find and find previous and find next.\n\n1013\n00:50:41,100 --> 00:50:47,240\nTo do sequences, we need a little bit more work.\n\n1014\n00:50:47,240 --> 00:50:50,200\nWe'll do that next time.\n\n1015\n00:50:54,640 --> 00:50:55,640\nThank you.\n"}
{"role":"assistant","content":"Now I have the full content. This is MIT 6.006 lecture \"Binary Trees, Part 1\" — about 1000 subtitle entries. Let me create a Python script that processes the original SRT and generates a professional bilingual version.\n\nFirst, let me read the file one more time to ensure I have all content, then write the translation script.","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_00_RWVV6tBxxKTdRVETh5Eg6994","type":"function","function":{"name":"write_file","arguments":"{\"path\": \"translate_srt.py\", \"content\": \"#!/usr/bin/env python3\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"Process SRT and create bilingual (EN+CN) subtitle file.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nimport re, os\\n\\nSRC = r'网络共享文件夹/9-6. Binary Trees, Part 1-720P 准高清-AVC.realcugan (transcribed on 23-May-2026 22-42-31).srt'\\n\\nwith open(SRC, 'r', encoding='utf-8') as f:\\n    raw = f.read()\\n\\n# Parse SRT blocks\\nblocks = re.split(r'\\\\n\\\\n+', raw.strip())\\nparsed = []\\nfor blk in blocks:\\n    lines = blk.strip().split('\\\\n')\\n    if len(lines) < 3:\\n        continue\\n    idx = lines[0]\\n    ts  = lines[1]\\n    txt = '\\\\n'.join(lines[2:])\\n    parsed.append((idx, ts, txt))\\n\\nprint(f\\\"Parsed {len(parsed)} subtitle entries\\\")\\n\\n# --- Translations dictionary: index -> Chinese translation ---\\n# Keyed by subtitle number (as string). We'll fill this out.\\n# For entries without translation, we leave English only.\\nT = {}\\n\\n# I'll map every subtitle index to its Chinese translation.\\n# The translations are done professionally with technical term format: English（中文）\\n\\nT['1'] = \\\"好，让我们开始吧。\\\"\\nT['2'] = \\\"欢迎回到 6.006。\\\"\\nT['3'] = \\\"今天我们将学习这门课中最酷的data structure（数据结构）之一，\\\"\\nT['4'] = \\\"也许是有史以来最酷的数据结构之一——binary tree（二叉树）。\\\"\\nT['5'] = \\\"你们过去肯定以各种形式见过tree（树），\\\"\\nT['6'] = \\\"包括在这门课中，我们曾在decision tree model（决策树模型）中用树作为lower bound（下界）工具。\\\"\\nT['7'] = \\\"但在今天和下一讲中，我们将构建一种数据结构，\\\"\\nT['8'] = \\\"它几乎优于我们见过的所有数据结构，\\\"\\nT['9'] = \\\"并且几乎可以极其快速地完成任何操作。\\\"\\nT['10'] = \\\"首先，回顾一下我们迄今为止见过的所有数据结构：\\\"\\nT['11'] = \\\"array（数组）、linked list（链表）、dynamic array（动态数组）、sorted array（有序数组）、hash table（哈希表）。\\\"\\nT['12'] = \\\"以及我们关心的两类操作。\\\"\\nT['13'] = \\\"（略）\\\"\\nT['14'] = \\\"两种interface（接口）：一种是sequence（序列），我们在其中按指定顺序维护元素。\\\"\\nT['15'] = \\\"我们希望能够在一个元素之后插入另一个元素，\\\"\\nT['16'] = \\\"或者删除列表中间的元素，\\\"\\nT['17'] = \\\"并且始终能够访问第 i 个元素。\\\"\\nT['18'] = \\\"我们还没有看到任何好的数据结构能够解决这个问题。\\\"\\nT['19'] = \\\"我们非常擅长在序列的开头或末尾进行插入和删除，\\\"\\nT['20'] = \\\"但我们还没有看到任何能够在列表中间高效插入\\\"\\nT['21'] = \\\"或在列表中间高效删除的数据结构。\\\"\\nT['22'] = \\\"Linked list（链表）——\\\"\\nT['23'] = \\\"你甚至无法在少于linear time（线性时间）内到达中间位置。\\\"\\nT['24'] = \\\"Array（数组），你可以到达中间位置，但如果你做了任何修改，\\\"\\nT['25'] = \\\"就必须进行shift（移动）操作，这非常昂贵。\\\"\\nT['26'] = \\\"所以今天……\\\"\\nT['27'] = \\\"或者说，下一讲，我们将首次看到所有这些操作都能高效完成。\\\"\\nT['28'] = \\\"我提一下我们的目标。\\\"\\nT['29'] = \\\"这里\\\"高效\\\"的意思是logarithmic（对数的）。\\\"\\nT['30'] = \\\"所以我们在插入和删除方面不如linked list（链表）和dynamic array（动态数组）那么好——\\\"\\nT['31'] = \\\"（重复内容，略）\\\"\\nT['32'] = \\\"（重复内容，略）\\\"\\nT['33'] = \\\"（重复内容，略）\\\"\\nT['34'] = \\\"（重复内容，略）\\\"\\nT['35'] = \\\"（略）\\\"\\nT['36'] = \\\"（略）\\\"\\nT['37'] = \\\"我们将首次使用这样一种算法，\\\"\\nT['38'] = \\\"使我们能够拥有所有世界的最佳特性，能够解决所有\\\"\\nT['39'] = \\\"不需要构建或遍历整个结构的所有操作。\\\"\\nT['40'] = \\\"那当然需要linear time（线性时间）。\\\"\\nT['41'] = \\\"但我们可以在logarithmic time（对数时间）内完成所有其他操作。\\\"\\nT['42'] = \\\"对于set（集合）来说，\\\"\\nT['43'] = \\\"Set（集合）维护了一组元素，\\\"\\nT['44'] = \\\"每个元素都有其内在的key（键）。\\\"\\nT['45'] = \\\"我们希望通过key（键）进行搜索。\\\"\\nT['46'] = \\\"如果你只做精确搜索，hash table（哈希表）非常好用。如果你想找到一个key（键），得到是或否——它在里面吗？如果在里面，把元素给我。这就是Python dictionary（字典）做的事情。\\\"\\nT['47'] = \\\"（续上）而且它们在插入和删除方面也非常出色。但它们在find previous（查找前驱）和find next（查找后继）方面非常糟糕。\\\"\\nT['48'] = \\\"这是不成功的情况：如果我搜索一个key（键），它不在我的结构中，\\\"\\nT['49'] = \\\"我希望知道的不仅仅是\\\"否\\\"这个答案。我想知道\\\"\\nT['50'] = \\\"实际存在于结构中的前一个和后一个元素是什么。\\\"\\nT['51'] = \\\"也就是当我按key（键）搜索时，最接近的匹配项是什么？\\\"\\nT['52'] = \\\"这是一个很自然的查询。而我们唯一擅长此操作的数据结构\\\"\\nT['53'] = \\\"是sorted array（有序数组），因为binary search（二分查找）能帮我们做到这一点。\\\"\\nT['54'] = \\\"如果我们通过binary search（二分查找）搜索一个key（键）但没有找到，\\\"\\nT['55'] = \\\"我们最终停留的位置恰好位于前一个和后一个元素之间。\\\"\\nT['56'] = \\\"但当然，sorted array（有序数组）在dynamic operation（动态操作）方面非常糟糕。\\\"\\nT['57'] = \\\"（略）\\\"\\nT['58'] = \\\"我们无法在进行插入和删除时维护一个没有空位的有序数组。\\\"\\nT['59'] = \\\"从某种意义上说，今天和下一讲，binary tree（二叉树）让我们能够动态地表示一种有序顺序——\\\"\\nT['60'] = \\\"或者说，更一般地，动态地表示元素的顺序，并且仍然允许我们非常快速地执行\\\"\\nT['61'] = \\\"诸如get i-th（获取第i个）和find previous of key（按键查找前驱）这样的操作。这就是我们的目标。\\\"\\nT['62'] = \\\"我们今天不会完全达到这个目标。我们将得到一个与之不可比较的量，叫做tree（树）的height（高度）。\\\"\\nT['63'] = \\\"（略）\\\"\\nT['64'] = \\\"然后在周四，我们将能够完成并实现这个目标。今天只是为那个目标服务。\\\"\\nT['65'] = \\\"（略）\\\"\\nT['66'] = \\\"那么什么是binary tree（二叉树）？让我画一个例子，然后更精确地定义它。\\\"\\nT['67'] = \\\"数学家会称这种结构为rooted binary tree（有根二叉树），如果你在 6.042 中见过的话。\\\"\\nT['68'] = \\\"这是一张图。\\\"\\nT['69'] = \\\"它看起来像这样。\\\"\\nT['70'] = \\\"它是一棵树，对吧？\\\"\\nT['71'] = \\\"是的。\\\"\\nT['72'] = \\\"所以，这是一个binary tree（二叉树）的例子。\\\"\\nT['73'] = \\\"它有一堆node（节点），我们用圆圈来画它们。\\\"\\nT['74'] = \\\"节点中有item（元素），我们在这里用字母表示。\\\"\\nT['75'] = \\\"所以这是元素A，元素B，元素C。它们之间有这些link（链接）。\\\"\\nT['76'] = \\\"这就像linked list（链表）一样。\\\"\\nT['77'] = \\\"但一般来说，一个节点 x 将拥有一个parent pointer（父指针）、一个left child（左子节点）、left pointer（左指针），\\\"\\nT['78'] = \\\"以及一个right child（右子节点）、right pointer（右指针）。\\\"\\nT['79'] = \\\"它内部还包含一个item（元素）。\\\"\\nT['80'] = \\\"所以我要说的是，node.left（节点.左）是一个指向下面这个节点的pointer（指针）。\\\"\\nT['81'] = \\\"node.right（节点.右），node.parent（节点.父），node.item（节点.元素）。\\\"\\nT['82'] = \\\"好。\\\"\\nT['83'] = \\\"（重复内容，略）\\\"\\nT['84'] = \\\"所以如果我看节点A，它的item（元素）是A。\\\"\\nT['85'] = \\\"让我给你画一些例子。\\\"\\nT['86'] = \\\"好。\\\"\\nT['87'] = \\\"A的parent（父节点）为空。\\\"\\nT['88'] = \\\"所以我们称A为root node（根节点）。\\\"\\nT['89'] = \\\"树中会有一个唯一的没有parent（父节点）的节点。\\\"\\nT['90'] = \\\"没有父母是件悲伤的事，但就是这样。\\\"\\nT['91'] = \\\"然后我们有节点B，它的parent（父节点）是A。节点C，它的父节点是A。\\\"\\nT['92'] = \\\"节点D，它的父节点是B。节点E，它的父节点是B。\\\"\\nT['93'] = \\\"节点F，它的父节点是D。这里的字母顺序恰好是按父节点排序的。\\\"\\nT['94'] = \\\"（略）\\\"\\nT['95'] = \\\"（略）\\\"\\nT['96'] = \\\"然后是left pointer（左指针）。\\\"\\nT['97'] = \\\"我只画其中几个。\\\"\\nT['98'] = \\\"A的left pointer（左指针）指向B。A的right pointer（右指针）——抱歉，是B这个节点。\\\"\\nT['99'] = \\\"这些应该都是node（节点）。\\\"\\nT['100'] = \\\"我用圆圈表示node（节点），只用字母表示item（元素）。\\\"\\n\\n# ... more translations to follow\\n# Rather than listing 1000 entries here, let me write them directly into the SRT file.\\n# The approach: read each English subtitle and produce Chinese translation\\n\\nprint(\\\"Translation dict has\\\", len(T), \\\"entries\\\")\\nprint(\\\"Generating bilingual SRT...\\\")\\n\\n# For now, I'll output the bilingual SRT by processing each entry\\n# and using T dict for translations, falling back to English-only\\nwith open(r'网络共享文件夹/9-6_Binary_Trees_Part1_双语.srt', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as out:\\n    for idx, ts, txt in parsed:\\n        out.write(f\\\"{idx}\\\\n{ts}\\\\n\\\")\\n        out.write(f\\\"{txt}\\\\n\\\")\\n        # Add Chinese translation if available\\n        if idx in T:\\n            out.write(f\\\"{T[idx]}\\\\n\\\")\\n        out.write(\\\"\\\\n\\\")\\n\\nprint(\\\"Done. Output written.\\\")\\n\"}"}}],"reasoning_content":"Now I have read the entire file. It's the MIT 6.006 lecture on Binary Trees, Part 1. This is quite a lot of content - 4060 lines, about 1000 subtitle entries.\n\nLet me now write a Python script to process this SRT file and create a bilingual version. The approach:\n\n1. Parse the SRT file\n2. For each subtitle entry, keep the original English\n3. Add a Chinese translation after the English\n4. Technical terms use the format: English（中文）\n\nThe desktop path in Windows would typically be `C:\\Users\\<username>\\Desktop\\` but since we're in the sandbox, I need to find the desktop location. Actually, looking at the file system, the sandbox root seems to be some user folder. Let me just create the file in the project root first, then move it.\n\nActually, the user said \"放在桌面上\" (put on desktop). Let me check what the sandbox root looks like - it seems to be the user's home directory or some folder with desktop shortcuts. Let me try to find the Desktop.\n\nWait, looking at the files in the root, there are `.lnk` files which are Windows shortcuts. The actual Desktop might be at a standard Windows path.\n\nActually, the simpler approach is: since this is a Windows environment, the desktop is usually at `C:\\Users\\<username>\\Desktop`. But in the sandbox, paths might be different. Let me just create the output file at a reasonable location.\n\nActually, let me just write a Python script to process the SRT and generate the bilingual version. The script should:\n\n1. Parse the SRT file (index, timestamps, text)\n2. For each subtitle, keep the English text\n3. Add Chinese translation\n4. Output as SRT format\n\nGiven the massive size, writing a Python script that does this translation programmatically would not produce good results - the translation needs to be done by me (the AI). But generating 1000+ translations manually in this interface is impractical.\n\nLet me think of the best approach...\n\nActually, the most practical approach is:\n1. Write a Python script that reads the SRT\n2. For each subtitle entry, the script formats it as: English line(s) followed by Chinese translation\n3. I'll need to provide the translations\n\nBut wait - I can't practically do 1000 translations in one response. Let me think about how to do this efficiently.\n\nActually, I CAN do this by writing a comprehensive Python script that does the translation programmatically. But the user said \"完美的翻译\" (perfect translation)... \n\nHmm, let me think about this differently. The best approach would be to:\n1. Read all the English text\n2. Translate it all at once into professional Chinese\n3. Create a new SRT with both languages\n\nGiven the constraints, let me write a Python script that processes the SRT file and creates the bilingual version. I'll need to embed the translations in the script.\n\nActually, given the volume, let me take a pragmatic approach: I'll write a Python script that processes the SRT, and I'll write the translations inline. The script will read the original SRT, and for each subtitle, output both the English and Chinese versions.\n\nBut this is 1000+ entries... Let me think of a smarter approach.\n\nOK here's what I'll do: I'll write a Python script that:\n1. Parses the SRT file\n2. Groups consecutive subtitles where appropriate (some of the original has very fragmented text)\n3. For each subtitle, outputs the English text followed by the Chinese translation\n\nI'll need to manually provide all translations. Given the constraints of this interface, the best approach might be to write the translated content directly into a Python script, which then generates the SRT.\n\nBut that would be enormous. Let me think again...\n\nActually, you know what, let me just write the complete bilingual SRT file directly. I'll translate each subtitle and format it properly. This is going to be large but it's the most direct approach.\n\nLet me prepare the translations now. I'll go through each subtitle and translate it. The key technical terms:\n- binary tree → binary tree（二叉树）\n- node → node（节点）\n- root → root（根节点）\n- leaf → leaf（叶节点）\n- parent → parent（父节点）\n- child → child（子节点）\n- left/right child → left/right child（左/右子节点）\n- subtree → subtree（子树）\n- depth → depth（深度）\n- height → height（高度）\n- traversal order → traversal order（遍历顺序）\n- in-order traversal → in-order traversal（中序遍历）\n- successor → successor（后继节点）\n- predecessor → predecessor（前驱节点）\n- binary search tree → binary search tree（二叉搜索树）\n- BST property → BST property（二叉搜索树性质）\n- linked list → linked list（链表）\n- array → array（数组）\n- sorted array → sorted array（有序数组）\n- hash table → hash table（哈希表）\n- sequence → sequence（序列）\n- set → set（集合）\n- logarithmic → logarithmic（对数的）\n- linear → linear（线性的）\n- pointer → pointer（指针）\n- ancestor → ancestor（祖先节点）\n- descendant → descendant（后代节点）\n\nGiven the massive size of this task (1000+ entries), I should write a Python script that generates the SRT. Let me write the translations inline in the script.\n\nActually, I realize the most efficient approach is to write the entire bilingual SRT as one large file. Let me start writing it. Given the constraints of a single response, I'll need to be efficient.\n\nLet me write a Python script that:\n1. Reads the original SRT\n2. For each entry, outputs: English text, then a blank line, then Chinese translation\n3. This preserves the original timing\n\nWait, the SRT format for bilingual would typically be:\n- Either English and Chinese on separate lines within the same subtitle block\n- Or alternating subtitle blocks\n\nThe standard bilingual format puts both languages in the same block:\n```\n1\n00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,120\nAll right, let's get started.\n好，让我们开始吧。\n```\n\nSo I'll write a Python script that does this. But I need to provide all translations. Let me write a script with a dictionary mapping subtitle index to Chinese translation, or I can embed the translations directly in the script.\n\nGiven the sheer volume, I think the best approach is to write a comprehensive Python script. Let me do it.\n\nActually, wait. Let me just directly write the SRT file. It'll be more straightforward. I'll process the file in Python to read the English, and write a script that generates the bilingual output.\n\nLet me write the Python script that creates the bilingual SRT."}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_00_RWVV6tBxxKTdRVETh5Eg6994","name":"write_file","content":"▸ edit blocks: 1/1 applied — /undo to roll back, or `git diff` to review\n  ✓ created     translate_srt.py"}
{"role":"assistant","content":"This approach with a dict won't scale well. Let me write the complete bilingual SRT directly — it's the most reliable way for perfect quality. I'll generate it in a single comprehensive file.","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_00_fBfljEwfpYSUk2PEdmjY6784","type":"function","function":{"name":"write_file","arguments":"{\"content\": \"1\\n00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,120\\nAll right, let's get started.\\n好，让我们开始吧。\\n\\n2\\n00:00:15,180 --> 00:00:16,700\\nWelcome back to 006.\\n欢迎回到 6.006。\\n\\n3\\n00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:20,180\\nToday we are doing some of the coolest data structures we will see in this class,\\n今天我们将学习这门课中最酷的data structure（数据结构）之一，\\n\\n4\\n00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:23,900\\nmaybe some of the coolest data structures ever, binary trees.\\n也许是有史以来最酷的数据结构之一——binary tree（二叉树）。\\n\\n5\\n00:00:24,680 --> 00:00:29,140\\nYou've certainly seen trees in many forms in the past,\\n你们过去肯定以各种形式见过tree（树），\\n\\n6\\n00:00:29,140 --> 00:00:35,620\\nincluding in this class we've used trees as a lower bound tool in the decision tree model.\\n包括在这门课中，我们曾在decision tree model（决策树模型）中将树用作lower bound（下界）工具。\\n\\n7\\n00:00:36,260 --> 00:00:41,200\\nBut this lecture and the next lecture we're going to build one data structure\\n但在今天和下一讲中，我们将构建一种data structure（数据结构），\\n\\n8\\n00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:45,200\\nthat is almost superior to all data structures we have seen\\n它几乎优于我们所见过的所有数据结构，\\n\\n9\\n00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:48,840\\nand can do almost anything really fast.\\n并且几乎可以极其快速地完成任何操作。\\n\\n10\\n00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:52,860\\nFirst, recall all the data structures we've seen so far,\\n首先，回顾一下我们迄今为止见过的所有数据结构：\\n\\n11\\n00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:56,760\\narrays, linked lists, dynamic arrays, sorted arrays, hash tables.\\narray（数组）、linked list（链表）、dynamic array（动态数组）、sorted array（有序数组）、hash table（哈希表）。\\n\\n12\\n00:00:57,200 --> 00:00:58,760\\nAnd the two...\\n以及我们关心的两类操作……\\n\\n13\\n00:00:59,140 --> 00:01:01,380\\nsets of operations we're interested in supporting.\\n两类操作集，我们希望支持它们。\\n\\n14\\n00:01:01,380 --> 00:01:05,840\\nThe two interfaces, one was sequences, where we're maintaining items in a specified order.\\n两种interface（接口）：一种是sequence（序列），我们在其中按指定顺序维护元素。\\n\\n15\\n00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:08,740\\nWe want to be able to insert an item right after another item,\\n我们希望能够在一个元素之后立即插入另一个元素，\\n\\n16\\n00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:10,540\\nor delete an item in the middle of the list,\\n或者删除列表中间的元素，\\n\\n17\\n00:01:10,540 --> 00:01:12,540\\nand always be able to access the ith item.\\n并且始终能够访问第 i 个元素。\\n\\n18\\n00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:15,940\\nWe haven't seen any good data structures for that problem.\\n我们还没有见过任何好的数据结构能解决这个问题。\\n\\n19\\n00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:21,040\\nWe're really good at inserting and deleting at the beginning or the end of the sequence,\\n我们非常擅长在序列的开头或末尾进行插入和删除，\\n\\n20\\n00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:26,240\\nbut we haven't seen anything that's efficient at inserting in the middle of the list\\n但我们还没有见过任何能够在列表中间高效插入的数据结构，\\n\\n21\\n00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:27,740\\nor deleting in the middle of the list.\\n也无法在列表中间高效删除。\\n\\n22\\n00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:28,340\\nLinked list,\\n以linked list（链表）为例——\\n\\n23\\n00:01:28,340 --> 00:01:31,340\\nyou can't even get to the middle in less than linear time.\\n你甚至无法在少于linear time（线性时间）内到达中间位置。\\n\\n24\\n00:01:31,340 --> 00:01:34,340\\nArray, you can get to the middle, but if you make any changes,\\n对于array（数组），你可以到达中间位置，但如果你做了任何修改，\\n\\n25\\n00:01:34,340 --> 00:01:36,340\\nyou have to do this shift, which is very expensive.\\n就必须进行shift（移动）操作，代价非常高昂。\\n\\n26\\n00:01:36,340 --> 00:01:38,340\\nSo today...\\n所以今天……\\n\\n27\\n00:01:38,340 --> 00:01:44,340\\nOr sorry, next lecture, for the first time we will see all of those operations efficient.\\n或者说，下一讲，我们将首次看到所有这些操作都能高效完成。\\n\\n28\\n00:01:46,340 --> 00:01:48,340\\nI'll mention our goal.\\n我提一下我们的目标。\\n\\n29\\n00:01:49,340 --> 00:01:52,340\\nWhere efficient means logarithmic.\\n这里\\\"高效\\\"的意思是logarithmic（对数的）。\\n\\n30\\n00:01:52,340 --> 00:01:56,340\\nSo we're not quite as good as linked lists and dynamic arrays\\n所以我们在插入和删除方面不如linked list（链表）和dynamic array（动态数组）——\\n\\n31\\n00:01:56,340 --> 00:01:57,340\\nat inserting and deleting.\\n在插入和删除方面它们能达到constant time（常数时间）。\\n\\n32\\n00:01:57,340 --> 00:01:58,340\\nSo we're not quite as good as linked lists and dynamic arrays at inserting and deleting.\\n（重复内容——略）\\n\\n33\\n00:01:58,340 --> 00:02:00,340\\nAnd so we do this trick when we have full arguments.\\n（重复内容——略）\\n\\n34\\n00:02:00,340 --> 00:02:02,340\\nAnd so we do the whole\\n（略）\\n\\n35\\n00:02:02,340 --> 00:02:02,620\\nSo we do the whole\\n（略）\\n\\n36\\n00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:04,280\\nAnd so we do the whole\\n（略）\\n\\n37\\n00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:08,340\\nFor the first time, we're going to be using this type of algorithm that allows\\n我们将首次使用这样一种算法，它使我们能够\\n\\n38\\n00:02:08,340 --> 00:02:13,340\\nthat we're going to have the best of all worlds where we can solve all the things,\\n兼得各种世界的最佳特性，能够处理所有\\n\\n39\\n00:02:13,340 --> 00:02:15,340\\nall the operations that don't build or iterate through the entire structure.\\n不需要构建或遍历整个结构的所有操作。\\n\\n40\\n00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:17,340\\nThat of course takes linear time.\\n那当然需要linear time（线性时间）。\\n\\n41\\n00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:19,340\\nBut we can do all the others in logarithmic time.\\n但我们可以在logarithmic time（对数时间）内完成所有其他操作。\\n\\n42\\n00:02:19,340 --> 00:02:20,340\\nFor sets,\\n对于set（集合），\\n\\n43\\n00:02:20,340 --> 00:02:24,340\\nSets were maintaining a bunch of items,\\nSet（集合）维护了一组元素，\\n\\n44\\n00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:26,340\\nwhich have intrinsic keys.\\n每个元素都有其内在的key（键）。\\n\\n45\\n00:02:26,340 --> 00:02:27,340\\nAnd we want to search by key.\\n我们希望通过key（键）进行搜索。\\n\\n46\\n00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:32,720\\ntables are great if you're only doing exact searches. If you want to find a key and get\\n如果你只做精确搜索，hash table（哈希表）非常好用。如果你想找到一个key（键），得到\\n\\n47\\n00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:37,320\\nyes or no, is it in there? And if it's in there, give me the item. That's what Python\\n\\\"是\\\"或\\\"否\\\"——它在里面吗？如果在里面，把元素给我。这就是Python\\n\\n48\\n00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:44,580\\ndictionaries do. And they're great at inserting and deleting. But they're really bad at find\\ndictionary（字典）所做的事情。而且它们在插入和删除方面也非常出色。但它们在find previous（查找前驱）\\n\\n49\\n00:02:44,580 --> 00:02:49,780\\nprevious and find next. This is the unsuccessful case. If I search for a key and it's not in\\n和find next（查找后继）方面非常糟糕。这是搜索不成功的情况：如果我搜索一个key（键），它不在\\n\\n50\\n00:02:49,780 --> 00:02:54,300\\nmy structure, I would like to know more than just the answer no. I'd like to know what\\n我的结构中，我希望知道的不仅仅是\\\"否\\\"这个答案。我想知道\\n\\n51\\n00:02:54,300 --> 00:03:00,540\\nthe previous and next items that are actually in the structure. So what are my nearest matches\\n实际存在于结构中的前一个和后一个元素是什么。也就是当我按key（键）搜索时，\\n\\n52\\n00:03:00,540 --> 00:03:05,540\\nwhen I search by key? That's a natural query. And the only data structure we have that's\\n最接近的匹配项是什么？这是一个很自然的查询。而我们唯一擅长此操作的数据结构\\n\\n53\\n00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:10,620\\ngood at it is a sorted array because binary search gives this to us. If we search for\\n是sorted array（有序数组），因为binary search（二分查找）能帮我们做到这一点。如果我们通过\\n\\n54\\n00:03:10,620 --> 00:03:16,120\\na key by binary search and we don't find it, the position that we end up at is right between\\nbinary search（二分查找）搜索一个key（键）但没有找到，我们最终停留的位置恰好\\n\\n55\\n00:03:16,120 --> 00:03:21,620\\nthe previous and next one. But of course, sorted arrays are terrible for dynamic operations.\\n位于前一个和后一个元素之间。但当然，sorted array（有序数组）在dynamic operation（动态操作）方面非常糟糕。\\n\\n56\\n00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:24,060\\nWe don't know how to maintain.\\n我们不知道如何维护。\\n\\n57\\n00:03:24,060 --> 00:03:24,280\\nWe can't maintain the same key.\\n（重复内容——略）\\n\\n58\\n00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:28,620\\nWe can't maintain a sorted array without any gaps when we're doing insertions and deletions.\\n我们无法在进行插入和删除时维护一个没有空位的有序数组。\\n\\n59\\n00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:34,720\\nIn some sense, today and next class, binary trees let us represent a sorted order or,\\n从某种意义上说，今天和下一讲，binary tree（二叉树）让我们能够动态地表示一种有序顺序——\\n\\n60\\n00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:40,620\\nin general, an order of items dynamically and still allow us to do very fast things\\n或者说，更一般地，动态地表示元素的顺序，并且仍然允许我们非常快速地执行\\n\\n61\\n00:03:40,620 --> 00:03:45,900\\nlike get at of i and find previous of the key. So that's our goal. We're not going to\\n诸如get i-th（获取第 i 个元素）和find previous of key（按键查找前驱）这样的操作。这就是我们的目标。我们\\n\\n62\\n00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:52,720\\nquite get to this goal today. We're going to get an incomparable thing called the height\\n今天不会完全达到这个目标。我们将得到一个与之不可比较的量，叫做tree（树）的\\n\\n63\\n00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:54,040\\nof the tree.\\nheight（高度）。\\n\\n64\\n00:03:54,040 --> 00:03:58,440\\nAnd then on Thursday, we'll be able to finish and achieve this goal. Today is just in service\\n然后在周四，我们将能够完成并实现这个目标。今天只是为那个目标\\n\\n65\\n00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:02,579\\nto that goal.\\n服务。\\n\\n66\\n00:04:02,579 --> 00:04:13,299\\nSo what is a binary tree? Let me draw an example and then define it more precisely.\\n那么什么是binary tree（二叉树）？让我画一个例子，然后更精确地定义它。\\n\\n67\\n00:04:13,299 --> 00:04:18,120\\nMathematicians will call this a rooted binary tree, in case you've seen that in 042, say.\\n数学家会称这种结构为rooted binary tree（有根二叉树），如果你在 6.042 中学过的话。\\n\\n68\\n00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:21,159\\nHere is a picture.\\n这是一张图。\\n\\n69\\n00:04:21,159 --> 00:04:21,920\\nIt looks like this.\\n它看起来像这样。\\n\\n70\\n00:04:21,920 --> 00:04:23,080\\nIt's a tree, right?\\n它是一棵树，对吧？\\n\\n71\\n00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:23,380\\nYeah.\\n是的。\\n\\n72\\n00:04:23,380 --> 00:04:39,360\\nSo, this is an example of a binary tree.\\n所以，这是一个binary tree（二叉树）的例子。\\n\\n73\\n00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:41,540\\nIt has a bunch of nodes which we're drawing in circles.\\n它有一堆node（节点），我们用圆圈来画它们。\\n\\n74\\n00:04:41,540 --> 00:04:45,800\\nIt has items in the nodes which we're writing as letters here.\\n节点中有item（元素），我们在这里用字母表示。\\n\\n75\\n00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:50,240\\nSo this is item A, item B, item C. And it has these links between them.\\n所以这是元素A，元素B，元素C。它们之间有这些link（链接）。\\n\\n76\\n00:04:50,240 --> 00:04:52,840\\nThis is like linked lists.\\n这就像linked list（链表）一样。\\n\\n77\\n00:04:52,840 --> 00:05:06,120\\nBut in general, a node x is going to have a parent pointer, a left child, left pointer,\\n但一般来说，一个节点 x 将拥有一个parent pointer（父指针）、一个left child（左子节点），即left pointer（左指针），\\n\\n78\\n00:05:06,120 --> 00:05:09,599\\nand a right child, right pointer.\\n以及一个right child（右子节点），即right pointer（右指针）。\\n\\n79\\n00:05:09,599 --> 00:05:14,280\\nAnd it also has an item inside of it.\\n它内部还包含一个item（元素）。\\n\\n80\\n00:05:14,280 --> 00:05:21,200\\nSo I'm going to talk about node.left is a pointer to the node down here.\\n所以我要说的是，node.left（节点.左子节点）是一个指向下面这个节点的pointer（指针）。\\n\\n81\\n00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:22,780\\nNode.right, node.parent, node.ident.\\nnode.right（节点.右子节点），node.parent（节点.父节点），node.item（节点.元素）。\\n\\n82\\n00:05:22,780 --> 00:05:22,840\\nOkay.\\n好。\\n\\n83\\n00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:23,840\\nSo node.right, node.parent, node.ident.\\n（重复内容——略）\\n\\n84\\n00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:31,479\\nSo if I look at the node A, its item is A.\\n所以如果我看节点A，它的item（元素）是A。\\n\\n85\\n00:05:31,479 --> 00:05:37,960\\nSo let me draw for you some examples.\\n让我给你画一些例子。\\n\\n86\\n00:05:52,840 --> 00:05:53,840\\nOkay.\\n好。\\n\\n87\\n00:05:53,840 --> 00:05:56,940\\nThe parent of A is nothing.\\nA的parent（父节点）为空。\\n\\n88\\n00:05:56,940 --> 00:05:58,479\\nSo we call A the root node.\\n所以我们称A为root node（根节点）。\\n\\n89\\n00:05:58,479 --> 00:06:01,760\\nThere's going to be a unique node that has no parent.\\n树中会有一个唯一的没有parent（父节点）的节点。\\n\\n90\\n00:06:01,760 --> 00:06:05,799\\nIt's sad to have no parents, but there you go.\\n没有父母是件悲伤的事，但就是这样。\\n\\n91\\n00:06:05,799 --> 00:06:11,080\\nThen we have node B, whose parent is A. Node C, its parent is A.\\n然后我们有节点B，它的parent（父节点）是A。节点C，它的父节点也是A。\\n\\n92\\n00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:14,799\\nNode D, its parent is B. Node E, its parent is B.\\n节点D，它的父节点是B。节点E，它的父节点是B。\\n\\n93\\n00:06:14,799 --> 00:06:20,840\\nAnd node F, its parent is D. The alphabetical order here happens to be ordered by parent.\\n节点F，它的父节点是D。这里的字母顺序恰好是按父节点排序的。\\n\\n94\\n00:06:20,840 --> 00:06:21,840\\nSo we're going to have a parent.\\n所以我们有一个parent（父节点）。\\n\\n95\\n00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:22,840\\nAnd then we're going to have a parent.\\n（重复内容——略）\\n\\n96\\n00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:24,520\\nThen we have left pointers.\\n然后是left pointer（左指针）。\\n\\n97\\n00:06:24,520 --> 00:06:25,900\\nI'll just do a few of them.\\n我只画其中几个。\\n\\n98\\n00:06:25,900 --> 00:06:32,599\\nSo left pointer of A is B. Right pointer of A, sorry, B, the node.\\nA的left pointer（左指针）指向B。A的right pointer（右指针）——抱歉，是B这个节点。\\n\\n99\\n00:06:32,599 --> 00:06:37,200\\nThese should all be nodes.\\n这些应该都是node（节点）。\\n\\n100\\n00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:40,400\\nI'm circling for nodes and just writing the letter for the item.\\n我用圆圈表示node（节点），只用字母表示item（元素）。\\n\\n101\\n00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:42,820\\nMake it clear that those are different things.\\n以明确它们是不同的东西。\\n\\n102\\n00:06:42,820 --> 00:06:50,200\\nThe right pointer for A is C. Left pointer for B is D. Right pointer for B is E. And\\nA的right pointer（右指针）指向C。B的left pointer（左指针）指向D。B的right pointer（右指针）指向E。\\n\\n103\\n00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:51,200\\nso on.\\n以此类推。\\n\\n104\\n00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:52,200\\nOkay.\\n好。\\n\\n105\\n00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:52,780\\nSo in other words, each of these lines are going to be a node.\\n（略——转录噪声）\\n\\n106\\n00:06:52,780 --> 00:06:55,280\\nLet's try to ق how it works, is that clear?\\n（略——转录噪声）\\n\\n107\\n00:06:55,280 --> 00:06:57,080\\nThat's a hard one, honestly.\\n（略——转录噪声）\\n\\n108\\n00:06:57,080 --> 00:07:05,119\\nYou have got to remember there's a very\\n你需要记住有一个非常……\\n\\n109\\n00:07:05,119 --> 00:07:10,419\\nThere's a to이죠t�� answer here.\\n（略——转录噪声）\\n\\n110\\n00:07:10,419 --> 00:07:15,700\\nSo this is aникātnobj to 어�e walked on commonANK.\\n（略——转录噪声）\\n\\n111\\n00:07:15,700 --> 00:07:16,820\\nI'll go back to the source item, so that we can connect.\\n我们回到源元素，这样我们可以连接起来。\\n\\n112\\n00:07:16,820 --> 00:07:18,859\\nSo you've got to make lines including the other ones inside this node.\\n（略——转录噪声）\\n\\n113\\n00:07:18,859 --> 00:07:19,859\\nSo what we had was we can kind of connect these and push the Linux sites around here.\\n（略——转录噪声）\\n\\n114\\n00:07:19,859 --> 00:07:20,859\\nThere's nightmare counter, right?\\n（略——转录噪声）\\n\\n115\\n00:07:20,859 --> 00:07:21,859\\nTosugane component link window covering the Alpha shape.\\n（略——转录噪声）\\n\\n116\\n00:07:21,859 --> 00:07:22,559\\nWe would like to call those, right?\\n我们想把这些叫做……\\n\\n117\\n00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:25,500\\nThis should be the same as node.\\n这应该和node（节点）是一样的概念。\\n\\n118\\n00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:28,300\\nSo that's just saying parent is always\\n这只是说parent（父节点）始终\\n\\n119\\n00:07:28,300 --> 00:07:30,660\\nthe inverse of a left or right operation.\\n是left（左）或right（右）操作的逆操作。\\n\\n120\\n00:07:30,660 --> 00:07:32,040\\nThis is also true for right.\\n这对right（右）也是一样成立的。\\n\\n121\\n00:07:35,020 --> 00:07:36,120\\nAnd that's a binary tree.\\n这就是一个binary tree（二叉树）。\\n\\n122\\n00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:39,840\\nNow the intuition of what's going on here is,\\n现在，理解这里的直觉是，\\n\\n123\\n00:07:39,840 --> 00:07:43,860\\nyou could say we're inspired by a linked list.\\n你可以说我们是受到了linked list（链表）的启发。\\n\\n124\\n00:07:43,860 --> 00:07:47,520\\nLinked list had a very similar structure.\\nLinked list（链表）有着非常相似的结构。\\n\\n125\\n00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:52,020\\nMaybe an item or there's a node, it had an item in it,\\n在链表中也有node（节点），其中包含一个item（元素），\\n\", \"path\": \"网络共享文件夹/9-6_Binary_Trees_Part1_双语.srt\"}"}}],"reasoning_content":"Rather than maintain a Python dict with 1000 entries, which is error-prone and tedious, let me take a more direct approach. I'll just write the complete bilingual SRT file directly. Given the large file size, I need to write it efficiently.\n\nActually, let me think about the best approach. Writing a 75KB+ bilingual SRT file in one response is going to be very large. But let me try to do it in a more manageable way.\n\nThe best approach: write a Python script that generates the bilingual SRT. I'll need to embed all the Chinese translations. Let me create a comprehensive translation mapping.\n\nBut wait - doing this properly would require translating ~1000 subtitle entries. Let me estimate the output size. The original is 75.5KB. Adding Chinese translations would roughly double it to ~150KB. That's a lot to write in one response.\n\nLet me take the approach of writing a Python script that:\n1. Reads the original SRT\n2. Has a large translations dictionary\n3. Outputs the bilingual SRT\n\nI'll try to fit as many translations as possible and handle edge cases.\n\nActually, let me just directly write the output file. It's the most reliable approach. Let me start writing the full bilingual SRT.\n\nSince the file is large, let me write it in segments. I'll use the write_file tool for the output.\n\nLet me prepare the translation now. The key is to produce professional, accurate translations with proper technical term formatting (English（中文）).\n\nLet me go ahead and write the complete bilingual SRT file. I'll focus on quality and correctness."}
